evidence_json:
[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-RET-003",
    "category": "Daily Retail & Orders",
    "question": "Can I pre-order a loaf for tomorrow?",
    "answer": "Pre-orders need 48 hours of notice because of our cold fermentation. For same-day or next-day needs, the counter holds a limited daily supply, which is first come, first served.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-RET-001",
    "category": "Daily Retail & Orders",
    "question": "What time do you usually sell out of Country Sourdough?",
    "answer": "Our daily counter supply generally sells out between 11:30 AM and 1:00 PM on weekdays, and often before 11:00 AM on weekends. We strongly recommend placing a pre-order through our website 48 hours in advance to guarantee your items.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Access Requirements:** Partners must supply a reliable keycode or lockbox key for entry. - **Drop Location:** Drops are made directly to the partner's designated walk-in cooler or food-safe staging counter. Fresh bread is placed in breathable paper or cloth bags, never sealed plastic. - **Cold Weather Protection:** From Nov 1 to Apr 1, exterior lockbox drops are strictly prohibited to prevent thermal shock. A staffed or interior access point is required. - **Refrigeration Warning:** Do not refrigerate bread. Cold storage speeds staling (see `BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md`)."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-002",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "How do I revive a stale loaf?",
    "answer": "Preheat your oven to 375\u00b0F (190\u00b0C). Run the unsliced loaf under cold water for 3 to 5 seconds, or spritz it well, then bake it directly on the center rack for 6 to 8 minutes. Rest for 5 minutes before slicing. It works best on loaves up to about 3 days old.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-003",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Can I freeze sourdough, and for how long?",
    "answer": "Yes. Slice the loaf, put parchment between slices, and seal it in a freezer bag at 0\u00b0F (-18\u00b0C). It keeps well for up to 3 months. Toast slices straight from frozen.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "1. **Preheat Oven:** Set the oven to **375°F (190°C)**. 2. **Moisten the Crust:** Run the entire unsliced stale loaf under cold tap water for 3 to 5 seconds, or spritz generously with a water bottle. Do not soak. 3. **Bake:** Place the wet loaf directly on the center oven rack for **6 to 8 minutes**. 4. **Rest:** Allow the loaf to rest for 5 minutes before slicing. Steam gelatinizes the interior crumb, which reverses some of the retrogradation, while the crust crisps. **Tips** - Use this for loaves up to about 3 days old. Beyond that the bread is best sliced and toasted, or made into croutons, breadcrumbs, or panzanella. - Half loaves: cut-side down, 4 to 5 minutes. - Revived bread is best eaten the same day, because it will stale faster the second time. - **Mold:** If you see any mold, discard the whole loaf. Do not cut around it."}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Commercial bread uses mono- and diglycerides and chemical preservatives. Cinder & Salt sourdough contains only flour, water, and sea salt. - **Starch Retrogradation:** Staling is not simply drying out. When bread cools after baking, the gelatinized starch molecules gradually recrystallize and push moisture out of the crumb, which makes it firm and leathery. - **The Refrigerator Trap:** Refrigeration temperatures (35°F–42°F / 1°C–5°C) **accelerate retrogradation by up to 300%**. *Never store fresh artisan bread in the refrigerator.* - **Why Sourdough Lasts Longer:** Natural acids from wild fermentation slow mold and retain moisture, which is why a high-hydration loaf like our Country Sourdough (78% hydration) usually stays pleasant for 4 to 5 days. - **Crust Moisture:** Crust softens as moisture migrates outward. That is normal, and it can be reversed (see Section 3)."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-002",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "How does the tasting box work, and what does it cost?",
    "answer": "A tasting box is $45.00 and includes 4 sourdough cake options and 3 seasonal buttercreams or preserves. Boxes are prepared on the 1st and 3rd Saturday of each month and are available for pickup from 10:00 AM to 1:00 PM. The fee is credited toward any final catering contract over $300.00.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Fee:** $45.00 per tasting box (includes 4 sourdough cake options and 3 seasonal buttercreams/preserves). - **Credit:** The $45.00 fee is credited toward any final catering contract exceeding **$300.00**. - **Pickup Schedule:** Boxes are prepared on the 1st and 3rd Saturday of every month and are available for pickup between 10:00 AM and 1:00 PM. - **Reservation:** Tasting boxes must be reserved at least 7 days in advance. Unclaimed boxes are not held past 1:00 PM. - **Allergen Note:** Tasting boxes are produced in our standard facility. Tree nuts, dairy, eggs, wheat, and sesame may be present. Our facility is **not celiac-safe**."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-RET-004",
    "category": "Daily Retail & Orders",
    "question": "How much does a Country Sourdough Boule cost?",
    "answer": "The Country Sourdough Boule (850 g baked weight, 78% hydration) is $9.50. Prices for other loaves and pastries are in our daily bake matrix. For example, the Seeded Dark Rye is $10.50, and all prices are subject to change.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Delivery Fee:** $35.00 within Zone A (Urban Core) and $65.00 within Zone B (Outer Metro). Locations beyond our zones are quoted individually. - **Delivery Window:** We deliver and set up during a 2-hour window agreed in your contract, finishing at least 90 minutes before service. - **Venue Requirements:** A level table, shade, and a clear loading path. A refrigerated space within the venue is strongly recommended for tiered cakes. - **Tiered Cakes:** Cakes of three or more tiers are assembled on site by a staff member, and an on-site fee of $100.00 applies. - **Equipment:** Stands, boards, and linens are provided on loan with a refundable $75.00 deposit. They must be returned within 5 days. - **Closed Mondays:** Delivery and pickup are not available on Mondays."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-WHO-004",
    "category": "Wholesale Accounts",
    "question": "What are your payment terms?",
    "answer": "Terms are Net-15 via automated ACH through the wholesale portal. Invoices generate when the delivery departs. New accounts need credit approval before the first delivery, and balances more than 15 days past due pause deliveries.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Credit Application:** New accounts submit a credit application and are approved before first delivery. Approval typically takes 3 business days. - **Payment Terms:** Net-15 via automated ACH through the wholesale portal. Invoices generate upon delivery departure. - **Late Payment:** Balances more than 15 days past due pause deliveries. A 1.5% monthly late fee applies after day 30. - **Quality Claims:** Missing, damaged, or unsatisfactory items must be reported within **12 hours of delivery drop** with cross-section photos. Approved claims are credited to the next invoice."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-WHO-002",
    "category": "Wholesale Accounts",
    "question": "What is the minimum order for wholesale delivery?",
    "answer": "The minimum order value is $75.00 per delivery drop. Orders below $75.00 incur a $15.00 small-drop surcharge.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Tier 1 – Standard:** Default wholesale pricing for all approved accounts. - **Tier 2 – Volume:** Applies once an account averages **$600.00 or more per week** over a trailing 4-week period. Example: Country Sourdough Boule is $6.25 at Tier 1 and $5.75 at Tier 2 (retail $9.50). - Current price lists live in the B2B Portal and follow `daily_bake_matrix.json`."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-001",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Should I keep my sourdough loaf in the refrigerator?",
    "answer": "Never refrigerate fresh artisan bread. Refrigerator temperatures (35\u00b0F-42\u00b0F / 1\u00b0C-5\u00b0C) accelerate starch retrogradation by up to 300%, causing the bread to dry out and go stale much faster. Store it cut-side down on a wooden board for days 1-2, then in a linen or paper bag for days 3-5.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "- **Days 1–2:** Place the loaf cut-side down directly on a wooden cutting board at room temperature. The cut face protects the crumb and the crust stays crisp. - **Days 3–5:** Transfer the loaf into an unbleached linen bread bag or brown paper sack. Avoid airtight plastic bags, which soften the crust and encourage mold. - **Location:** Keep away from the stove, direct sun, and humid spots. Ideal room temperature is 65°F–72°F (18°C–22°C)."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-003",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Can I freeze sourdough, and for how long?",
    "answer": "Yes. Slice the loaf, put parchment between slices, and seal it in a freezer bag at 0\u00b0F (-18\u00b0C). It keeps well for up to 3 months. Toast slices straight from frozen.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Commercial bread uses mono- and diglycerides and chemical preservatives. Cinder & Salt sourdough contains only flour, water, and sea salt. - **Starch Retrogradation:** Staling is not simply drying out. When bread cools after baking, the gelatinized starch molecules gradually recrystallize and push moisture out of the crumb, which makes it firm and leathery. - **The Refrigerator Trap:** Refrigeration temperatures (35°F–42°F / 1°C–5°C) **accelerate retrogradation by up to 300%**. *Never store fresh artisan bread in the refrigerator.* - **Why Sourdough Lasts Longer:** Natural acids from wild fermentation slow mold and retain moisture, which is why a high-hydration loaf like our Country Sourdough (78% hydration) usually stays pleasant for 4 to 5 days. - **Crust Moisture:** Crust softens as moisture migrates outward. That is normal, and it can be reversed (see Section 3)."}]

