[{"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Delivery Fee:** $35.00 within Zone A (Urban Core) and $65.00 within Zone B (Outer Metro). Locations beyond our zones are quoted individually. - **Delivery Window:** We deliver and set up during a 2-hour window agreed in your contract, finishing at least 90 minutes before service. - **Venue Requirements:** A level table, shade, and a clear loading path. A refrigerated space within the venue is strongly recommended for tiered cakes. - **Tiered Cakes:** Cakes of three or more tiers are assembled on site by a staff member, and an on-site fee of $100.00 applies. - **Equipment:** Stands, boards, and linens are provided on loan with a refundable $75.00 deposit. They must be returned within 5 days. - **Closed Mondays:** Delivery and pickup are not available on Mondays."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-001",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "How far in advance do I need to book a wedding cake or dessert table?",
    "answer": "We require a minimum of 6 weeks notice for wedding orders and large event catering (100+ guests), 14 days for smaller custom celebratory cakes, and 7 days for small wholesale event trays. Peak wedding season dates (May-October) can close up to 6 months ahead, so early booking is recommended.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-003",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "What are your deposit and cancellation terms for events?",
    "answer": "A 50% non-refundable deposit is due at contract signing, and the balance is due 14 days before the event. If you cancel 30 or more days ahead we keep the deposit. If you cancel 14-29 days ahead we retain 50% of the contract value, and under 14 days we retain 100%.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Weddings & Large Events (100+ guests):** Minimum **6 weeks** notice required. Peak-season dates (May–October) close up to 6 months in advance. - **Bespoke Celebratory Cakes:** Minimum **14 days** notice required. - **Small Wholesale Event Trays:** Minimum **7 days** notice required. - **Rush Requests:** We cannot accommodate bookings inside these windows. Our sourdough cake layers and laminated doughs need 18–36 hours of fermentation, and decorating takes more time on top of that."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-006",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "My child had a reaction after eating one of your products. What should I do?",
    "answer": "Please seek medical attention immediately, and call 911 if there is any trouble breathing, swelling, or other signs of anaphylaxis. Once your child is safe, contact us with the product name, purchase time, and any batch code so our Quality Lead can investigate and quarantine the batch.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-004",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Do you have a nut-free or sesame-free bread?",
    "answer": "Several breads contain no nut or sesame ingredients, such as the Country Sourdough and Spelt Boule. We bake plain loaves first, at 2:00 AM, before seeded and nut items at 4:00 AM, and we sanitize at 12:00 PM and 8:00 PM. Shared ovens and airborne particles mean we cannot guarantee a nut-free or sesame-free product, so people with severe allergies should not rely on us.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "1. **Slice first:** Slice the whole loaf on the day you buy it, or the day after. 2. **Separate:** Place parchment squares between slices so you can take out only what you need. 3. **Seal:** Place in a sealed freezer bag and press out as much air as possible. 4. **Freeze:** Store at 0°F (-18°C), and label with the date. 5. **Use:** Toast slices straight from frozen at medium heat. For a whole frozen loaf, thaw on the counter for 2 to 3 hours, then use the refresh technique below. *Note: Enriched pastries (croissants, Danishes, morning buns) are best eaten the same day. Freeze them within 24 hours and reheat at 350°F (177°C) for 8 to 10 minutes.*"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "1. **Preheat Oven:** Set the oven to **375°F (190°C)**. 2. **Moisten the Crust:** Run the entire unsliced stale loaf under cold tap water for 3 to 5 seconds, or spritz generously with a water bottle. Do not soak. 3. **Bake:** Place the wet loaf directly on the center oven rack for **6 to 8 minutes**. 4. **Rest:** Allow the loaf to rest for 5 minutes before slicing. Steam gelatinizes the interior crumb, which reverses some of the retrogradation, while the crust crisps. **Tips** - Use this for loaves up to about 3 days old. Beyond that the bread is best sliced and toasted, or made into croutons, breadcrumbs, or panzanella. - Half loaves: cut-side down, 4 to 5 minutes. - Revived bread is best eaten the same day, because it will stale faster the second time. - **Mold:** If you see any mold, discard the whole loaf. Do not cut around it."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-006",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "My child had a reaction after eating one of your products. What should I do?",
    "answer": "Please seek medical attention immediately, and call 911 if there is any trouble breathing, swelling, or other signs of anaphylaxis. Once your child is safe, contact us with the product name, purchase time, and any batch code so our Quality Lead can investigate and quarantine the batch.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-005",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "I have a wheat allergy. Is spelt or einkorn bread okay for me?",
    "answer": "No. Spelt and einkorn are ancient wheats and contain gluten and wheat proteins. They are not safe for people with a wheat allergy or celiac disease. We can't offer medical advice, so please check with your allergist.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Our products contain wheat or rye and may contain traces of tree nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs. Our facility is **not celiac-safe**. See `ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md`."}, {"source": "ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md", "quote": "- Every retail item carries a label with ingredients and a "Contains" statement. - A posted notice at the counter and on the website reads: *"Our facility is not celiac-safe. All items may contain traces of wheat, tree nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs."* - Customers with allergies are never told an item is "safe". They are told what is in it and what is nearby. - Any question we cannot answer from the allergen matrix is passed to the Head Baker, and no guess is offered."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-004",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Do you have a nut-free or sesame-free bread?",
    "answer": "Several breads contain no nut or sesame ingredients, such as the Country Sourdough and Spelt Boule. We bake plain loaves first, at 2:00 AM, before seeded and nut items at 4:00 AM, and we sanitize at 12:00 PM and 8:00 PM. Shared ovens and airborne particles mean we cannot guarantee a nut-free or sesame-free product, so people with severe allergies should not rely on us.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-002",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Which major allergens are present in your facility?",
    "answer": "Wheat/gluten, rye, tree nuts (walnuts and almonds), sesame, dairy (butter, milk, cheese), and eggs are all handled in our facility. Nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs use color-coded dedicated tools, but ovens, mixers, and the mill are shared, so cross-contact is possible. We do not guarantee any item is free of these allergens.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "We cannot guarantee any event item is free of wheat, tree nuts, sesame, dairy, or eggs. Please share all guest allergies in your intake form. We will give you full ingredient lists and cross-contact notes so you can plan, and we will tell you plainly if an item is not appropriate for a guest. See `ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md`."}]

[{"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Commercial bread uses mono- and diglycerides and chemical preservatives. Cinder & Salt sourdough contains only flour, water, and sea salt. - **Starch Retrogradation:** Staling is not simply drying out. When bread cools after baking, the gelatinized starch molecules gradually recrystallize and push moisture out of the crumb, which makes it firm and leathery. - **The Refrigerator Trap:** Refrigeration temperatures (35°F–42°F / 1°C–5°C) **accelerate retrogradation by up to 300%**. *Never store fresh artisan bread in the refrigerator.* - **Why Sourdough Lasts Longer:** Natural acids from wild fermentation slow mold and retain moisture, which is why a high-hydration loaf like our Country Sourdough (78% hydration) usually stays pleasant for 4 to 5 days. - **Crust Moisture:** Crust softens as moisture migrates outward. That is normal, and it can be reversed (see Section 3)."}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "- **Days 1–2:** Place the loaf cut-side down directly on a wooden cutting board at room temperature. The cut face protects the crumb and the crust stays crisp. - **Days 3–5:** Transfer the loaf into an unbleached linen bread bag or brown paper sack. Avoid airtight plastic bags, which soften the crust and encourage mold. - **Location:** Keep away from the stove, direct sun, and humid spots. Ideal room temperature is 65°F–72°F (18°C–22°C)."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-003",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Can I freeze sourdough, and for how long?",
    "answer": "Yes. Slice the loaf, put parchment between slices, and seal it in a freezer bag at 0\u00b0F (-18\u00b0C). It keeps well for up to 3 months. Toast slices straight from frozen.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-001",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Should I keep my sourdough loaf in the refrigerator?",
    "answer": "Never refrigerate fresh artisan bread. Refrigerator temperatures (35\u00b0F-42\u00b0F / 1\u00b0C-5\u00b0C) accelerate starch retrogradation by up to 300%, causing the bread to dry out and go stale much faster. Store it cut-side down on a wooden board for days 1-2, then in a linen or paper bag for days 3-5.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-006",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "My child had a reaction after eating one of your products. What should I do?",
    "answer": "Please seek medical attention immediately, and call 911 if there is any trouble breathing, swelling, or other signs of anaphylaxis. Once your child is safe, contact us with the product name, purchase time, and any batch code so our Quality Lead can investigate and quarantine the batch.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-002",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Which major allergens are present in your facility?",
    "answer": "Wheat/gluten, rye, tree nuts (walnuts and almonds), sesame, dairy (butter, milk, cheese), and eggs are all handled in our facility. Nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs use color-coded dedicated tools, but ovens, mixers, and the mill are shared, so cross-contact is possible. We do not guarantee any item is free of these allergens.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md", "quote": "1. **Halt Production:** Flag the affected batch with RED "ALLERGEN CONTAMINATED" tape and stop the line. 2. **Log Incident:** Record batch number, time, and cross-contact source in the Kitchen Shift Log. 3. **Repurpose or Dispose:** The batch cannot be sold under standard labeling. Relabel it internally as staff product with a full allergen declaration, or discard it. 4. **Notification:** Inform the Head Baker and Quality Lead before shift conclusion. 5. **Customer-Facing Incident (Reported Reaction):** - Advise the customer to seek medical attention immediately, and call 911 for breathing difficulty or anaphylaxis. - Record the product, purchase time, and batch code. - Escalate to the Quality Lead within 1 hour. Pull and quarantine remaining units from the same batch. 6. **Root-Cause Review:** Within 72 hours, the Quality Lead documents the cause, the corrective action, and any retraining."}, {"source": "ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md", "quote": "- Every retail item carries a label with ingredients and a "Contains" statement. - A posted notice at the counter and on the website reads: *"Our facility is not celiac-safe. All items may contain traces of wheat, tree nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs."* - Customers with allergies are never told an item is "safe". They are told what is in it and what is nearby. - Any question we cannot answer from the allergen matrix is passed to the Head Baker, and no guess is offered."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-003",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "What are your deposit and cancellation terms for events?",
    "answer": "A 50% non-refundable deposit is due at contract signing, and the balance is due 14 days before the event. If you cancel 30 or more days ahead we keep the deposit. If you cancel 14-29 days ahead we retain 50% of the contract value, and under 14 days we retain 100%.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Delivery Fee:** $35.00 within Zone A (Urban Core) and $65.00 within Zone B (Outer Metro). Locations beyond our zones are quoted individually. - **Delivery Window:** We deliver and set up during a 2-hour window agreed in your contract, finishing at least 90 minutes before service. - **Venue Requirements:** A level table, shade, and a clear loading path. A refrigerated space within the venue is strongly recommended for tiered cakes. - **Tiered Cakes:** Cakes of three or more tiers are assembled on site by a staff member, and an on-site fee of $100.00 applies. - **Equipment:** Stands, boards, and linens are provided on loan with a refundable $75.00 deposit. They must be returned within 5 days. - **Closed Mondays:** Delivery and pickup are not available on Mondays."}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Weddings & Large Events (100+ guests):** Minimum **6 weeks** notice required. Peak-season dates (May–October) close up to 6 months in advance. - **Bespoke Celebratory Cakes:** Minimum **14 days** notice required. - **Small Wholesale Event Trays:** Minimum **7 days** notice required. - **Rush Requests:** We cannot accommodate bookings inside these windows. Our sourdough cake layers and laminated doughs need 18–36 hours of fermentation, and decorating takes more time on top of that."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-001",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "How far in advance do I need to book a wedding cake or dessert table?",
    "answer": "We require a minimum of 6 weeks notice for wedding orders and large event catering (100+ guests), 14 days for smaller custom celebratory cakes, and 7 days for small wholesale event trays. Peak wedding season dates (May-October) can close up to 6 months ahead, so early booking is recommended.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}]

[{"source": "ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md", "quote": "- Every retail item carries a label with ingredients and a "Contains" statement. - A posted notice at the counter and on the website reads: *"Our facility is not celiac-safe. All items may contain traces of wheat, tree nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs."* - Customers with allergies are never told an item is "safe". They are told what is in it and what is nearby. - Any question we cannot answer from the allergen matrix is passed to the Head Baker, and no guess is offered."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-002",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Which major allergens are present in your facility?",
    "answer": "Wheat/gluten, rye, tree nuts (walnuts and almonds), sesame, dairy (butter, milk, cheese), and eggs are all handled in our facility. Nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs use color-coded dedicated tools, but ovens, mixers, and the mill are shared, so cross-contact is possible. We do not guarantee any item is free of these allergens.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-001",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Are your sourdough breads safe for someone with Celiac Disease?",
    "answer": "No. While our 24-36 hour wild fermentation process breaks down some starches and makes bread easier to digest for many gluten-sensitive people, our facility actively stone-mills grains on-site. Airborne flour particles are present throughout the bakery. We are NOT a certified celiac-safe environment, and we do not make certified gluten-free products.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Our facility is **not celiac-safe**, and we mill on-site. Partners reselling our products must pass along our allergen statement (see `ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md`) and must not describe our products as gluten-free or allergen-free."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-003",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "What is in your sourdough bread?",
    "answer": "Our core breads contain only flour (stone-milled in-house or from regional growers), water, wild sourdough starter, and sea salt. We use no commercial yeast, dough conditioners, preservatives, or artificial stabilizers. Seeded and enriched items add ingredients such as sesame, walnuts, cherries, or honey, and each item's label lists everything in it.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-001",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Are your sourdough breads safe for someone with Celiac Disease?",
    "answer": "No. While our 24-36 hour wild fermentation process breaks down some starches and makes bread easier to digest for many gluten-sensitive people, our facility actively stone-mills grains on-site. Airborne flour particles are present throughout the bakery. We are NOT a certified celiac-safe environment, and we do not make certified gluten-free products.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md", "quote": "Cinder & Salt Bakery operates an open-concept bakery and an active stone mill. We mill whole grains (wheat, rye, spelt, einkorn, Red Fife) on-site every week. - **Gluten Presence:** Fine flour particles remain airborne throughout the facility 24 hours a day. No wall, bench, or schedule fully removes them. - **Celiac Disclaimer:** While some items are prepared without gluten-containing ingredients, **our facility is NOT celiac-safe**. We do not produce certified gluten-free products. Customers with Celiac Disease, severe gluten intolerance, or wheat allergy should not consume our products. - **Fermentation Is Not a Safety Claim:** Our 24–36 hour cold fermentation breaks down some starches and phytic acid and makes bread gentler for many people. It does **not** reduce gluten to a celiac-safe level (the regulatory threshold is under 20 ppm). We never market it as doing so. - **Staff Script:** "We're an active stone mill, so flour is in the air all day. We can't make a celiac-safe promise, and we'd rather tell you that honestly than risk your health.""}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "**Cinder & Salt Bakery** — *Wild Yeast. Heritage Grain. Honest Craft.* Our loaves contain only flour, water, and sea salt, with no preservatives. That is what makes them honest, and it also means they need a little care."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-RET-003",
    "category": "Daily Retail & Orders",
    "question": "Can I pre-order a loaf for tomorrow?",
    "answer": "Pre-orders need 48 hours of notice because of our cold fermentation. For same-day or next-day needs, the counter holds a limited daily supply, which is first come, first served.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-RET-002",
    "category": "Daily Retail & Orders",
    "question": "What days are you open?",
    "answer": "We bake and sell Tuesday through Sunday. We are closed on Mondays, which is our rest and fermentation prep day, and there is no retail, delivery, or pickup on Mondays.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Our sourdough breads undergo a mandatory 24-to-36-hour cold fermentation cycle. Because we use no commercial yeast or dough accelerants, **all wholesale orders require a strict 48-hour advance notice**. - **Standing Order Freeze:** Standing orders lock at **2:00 PM** two days prior to delivery. - **Order Modifications:** Additions or reductions must be submitted through the B2B Portal before the 2:00 PM cutoff. Late modifications cannot be accommodated, because the dough is already mixed and committed. - **No Same-Day or Next-Day Orders:** We do not offer rush orders at any price. | Order Placed By (2:00 PM) | Fermentation & Proofing Window | Delivery Window | | :--- | :--- | :--- | | **Sunday** | Monday | **Tuesday Morning** | | **Monday** | Tuesday | **Wednesday Morning** | | **Tuesday** | Wednesday | **Thursday Morning** | | **Wednesday** | Thursday | **Friday Morning** | | **Thursday** | Friday | **Saturday Morning** | | **Friday** | Saturday | **Sunday Morning** | *Table Summary:* [1. The 48-Hour Fermentation Lead Time Rule] - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Sunday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Monday | Delivery Window: Tuesday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Monday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Tuesday | Delivery Window: Wednesday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Tuesday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Wednesday | Delivery Window: Thursday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Wednesday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Thursday | Delivery Window: Friday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Thursday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Friday | Delivery Window: Saturday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Friday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Saturday | Delivery Window: Sunday Morning *Note: Retail counter and delivery routes are closed on Mondays. Orders placed on Saturday before 2:00 PM are treated as placed for Monday and are not deliverable. They roll to the Sunday cutoff and deliver Tuesday.*"}]

