[{"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Delivery Fee:** $35.00 within Zone A (Urban Core) and $65.00 within Zone B (Outer Metro). Locations beyond our zones are quoted individually. - **Delivery Window:** We deliver and set up during a 2-hour window agreed in your contract, finishing at least 90 minutes before service. - **Venue Requirements:** A level table, shade, and a clear loading path. A refrigerated space within the venue is strongly recommended for tiered cakes. - **Tiered Cakes:** Cakes of three or more tiers are assembled on site by a staff member, and an on-site fee of $100.00 applies. - **Equipment:** Stands, boards, and linens are provided on loan with a refundable $75.00 deposit. They must be returned within 5 days. - **Closed Mondays:** Delivery and pickup are not available on Mondays."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-002",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "How does the tasting box work, and what does it cost?",
    "answer": "A tasting box is $45.00 and includes 4 sourdough cake options and 3 seasonal buttercreams or preserves. Boxes are prepared on the 1st and 3rd Saturday of each month and are available for pickup from 10:00 AM to 1:00 PM. The fee is credited toward any final catering contract over $300.00.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Fee:** $45.00 per tasting box (includes 4 sourdough cake options and 3 seasonal buttercreams/preserves). - **Credit:** The $45.00 fee is credited toward any final catering contract exceeding **$300.00**. - **Pickup Schedule:** Boxes are prepared on the 1st and 3rd Saturday of every month and are available for pickup between 10:00 AM and 1:00 PM. - **Reservation:** Tasting boxes must be reserved at least 7 days in advance. Unclaimed boxes are not held past 1:00 PM. - **Allergen Note:** Tasting boxes are produced in our standard facility. Tree nuts, dairy, eggs, wheat, and sesame may be present. Our facility is **not celiac-safe**."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-001",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "How far in advance do I need to book a wedding cake or dessert table?",
    "answer": "We require a minimum of 6 weeks notice for wedding orders and large event catering (100+ guests), 14 days for smaller custom celebratory cakes, and 7 days for small wholesale event trays. Peak wedding season dates (May-October) can close up to 6 months ahead, so early booking is recommended.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-003",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "What is in your sourdough bread?",
    "answer": "Our core breads contain only flour (stone-milled in-house or from regional growers), water, wild sourdough starter, and sea salt. We use no commercial yeast, dough conditioners, preservatives, or artificial stabilizers. Seeded and enriched items add ingredients such as sesame, walnuts, cherries, or honey, and each item's label lists everything in it.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-004",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Do you have a nut-free or sesame-free bread?",
    "answer": "Several breads contain no nut or sesame ingredients, such as the Country Sourdough and Spelt Boule. We bake plain loaves first, at 2:00 AM, before seeded and nut items at 4:00 AM, and we sanitize at 12:00 PM and 8:00 PM. Shared ovens and airborne particles mean we cannot guarantee a nut-free or sesame-free product, so people with severe allergies should not rely on us.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md", "quote": "- Every retail item carries a label with ingredients and a "Contains" statement. - A posted notice at the counter and on the website reads: *"Our facility is not celiac-safe. All items may contain traces of wheat, tree nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs."* - Customers with allergies are never told an item is "safe". They are told what is in it and what is nearby. - Any question we cannot answer from the allergen matrix is passed to the Head Baker, and no guess is offered."}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Our facility is **not celiac-safe**, and we mill on-site. Partners reselling our products must pass along our allergen statement (see `ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md`) and must not describe our products as gluten-free or allergen-free."}]

[{"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Commercial bread uses mono- and diglycerides and chemical preservatives. Cinder & Salt sourdough contains only flour, water, and sea salt. - **Starch Retrogradation:** Staling is not simply drying out. When bread cools after baking, the gelatinized starch molecules gradually recrystallize and push moisture out of the crumb, which makes it firm and leathery. - **The Refrigerator Trap:** Refrigeration temperatures (35°F–42°F / 1°C–5°C) **accelerate retrogradation by up to 300%**. *Never store fresh artisan bread in the refrigerator.* - **Why Sourdough Lasts Longer:** Natural acids from wild fermentation slow mold and retain moisture, which is why a high-hydration loaf like our Country Sourdough (78% hydration) usually stays pleasant for 4 to 5 days. - **Crust Moisture:** Crust softens as moisture migrates outward. That is normal, and it can be reversed (see Section 3)."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-001",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Should I keep my sourdough loaf in the refrigerator?",
    "answer": "Never refrigerate fresh artisan bread. Refrigerator temperatures (35\u00b0F-42\u00b0F / 1\u00b0C-5\u00b0C) accelerate starch retrogradation by up to 300%, causing the bread to dry out and go stale much faster. Store it cut-side down on a wooden board for days 1-2, then in a linen or paper bag for days 3-5.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-003",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Can I freeze sourdough, and for how long?",
    "answer": "Yes. Slice the loaf, put parchment between slices, and seal it in a freezer bag at 0\u00b0F (-18\u00b0C). It keeps well for up to 3 months. Toast slices straight from frozen.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "1. **Preheat Oven:** Set the oven to **375°F (190°C)**. 2. **Moisten the Crust:** Run the entire unsliced stale loaf under cold tap water for 3 to 5 seconds, or spritz generously with a water bottle. Do not soak. 3. **Bake:** Place the wet loaf directly on the center oven rack for **6 to 8 minutes**. 4. **Rest:** Allow the loaf to rest for 5 minutes before slicing. Steam gelatinizes the interior crumb, which reverses some of the retrogradation, while the crust crisps. **Tips** - Use this for loaves up to about 3 days old. Beyond that the bread is best sliced and toasted, or made into croutons, breadcrumbs, or panzanella. - Half loaves: cut-side down, 4 to 5 minutes. - Revived bread is best eaten the same day, because it will stale faster the second time. - **Mold:** If you see any mold, discard the whole loaf. Do not cut around it."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-003",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "What are your deposit and cancellation terms for events?",
    "answer": "A 50% non-refundable deposit is due at contract signing, and the balance is due 14 days before the event. If you cancel 30 or more days ahead we keep the deposit. If you cancel 14-29 days ahead we retain 50% of the contract value, and under 14 days we retain 100%.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Deposit:** 50% non-refundable deposit required at contract signing to reserve the event date. - **Final Balance:** Remaining balance must be paid in full 14 days prior to the event date. - **Cancellations:** - 30+ days prior: deposit retained. - 14–29 days prior: 50% of contract value retained. - Under 14 days: 100% of contract value retained. - **Guest Count Changes:** Final guest count is due 14 days before the event. It can increase by up to 10% within that window, subject to capacity. It cannot decrease after that date."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-001",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "How far in advance do I need to book a wedding cake or dessert table?",
    "answer": "We require a minimum of 6 weeks notice for wedding orders and large event catering (100+ guests), 14 days for smaller custom celebratory cakes, and 7 days for small wholesale event trays. Peak wedding season dates (May-October) can close up to 6 months ahead, so early booking is recommended.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Weddings & Large Events (100+ guests):** Minimum **6 weeks** notice required. Peak-season dates (May–October) close up to 6 months in advance. - **Bespoke Celebratory Cakes:** Minimum **14 days** notice required. - **Small Wholesale Event Trays:** Minimum **7 days** notice required. - **Rush Requests:** We cannot accommodate bookings inside these windows. Our sourdough cake layers and laminated doughs need 18–36 hours of fermentation, and decorating takes more time on top of that."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-WHO-004",
    "category": "Wholesale Accounts",
    "question": "What are your payment terms?",
    "answer": "Terms are Net-15 via automated ACH through the wholesale portal. Invoices generate when the delivery departs. New accounts need credit approval before the first delivery, and balances more than 15 days past due pause deliveries.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Credit Application:** New accounts submit a credit application and are approved before first delivery. Approval typically takes 3 business days. - **Payment Terms:** Net-15 via automated ACH through the wholesale portal. Invoices generate upon delivery departure. - **Late Payment:** Balances more than 15 days past due pause deliveries. A 1.5% monthly late fee applies after day 30. - **Quality Claims:** Missing, damaged, or unsatisfactory items must be reported within **12 hours of delivery drop** with cross-section photos. Approved claims are credited to the next invoice."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-WHO-003",
    "category": "Wholesale Accounts",
    "question": "When do you deliver, and which zones do you serve?",
    "answer": "We deliver Tuesday through Sunday between 4:30 AM and 7:00 AM. Zone A (Urban Core) receives daily drops, and Zone B (Outer Metro) receives drops on Tuesday, Thursday, and Saturday.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Tier 1 – Standard:** Default wholesale pricing for all approved accounts. - **Tier 2 – Volume:** Applies once an account averages **$600.00 or more per week** over a trailing 4-week period. Example: Country Sourdough Boule is $6.25 at Tier 1 and $5.75 at Tier 2 (retail $9.50). - Current price lists live in the B2B Portal and follow `daily_bake_matrix.json`."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-001",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Should I keep my sourdough loaf in the refrigerator?",
    "answer": "Never refrigerate fresh artisan bread. Refrigerator temperatures (35\u00b0F-42\u00b0F / 1\u00b0C-5\u00b0C) accelerate starch retrogradation by up to 300%, causing the bread to dry out and go stale much faster. Store it cut-side down on a wooden board for days 1-2, then in a linen or paper bag for days 3-5.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Commercial bread uses mono- and diglycerides and chemical preservatives. Cinder & Salt sourdough contains only flour, water, and sea salt. - **Starch Retrogradation:** Staling is not simply drying out. When bread cools after baking, the gelatinized starch molecules gradually recrystallize and push moisture out of the crumb, which makes it firm and leathery. - **The Refrigerator Trap:** Refrigeration temperatures (35°F–42°F / 1°C–5°C) **accelerate retrogradation by up to 300%**. *Never store fresh artisan bread in the refrigerator.* - **Why Sourdough Lasts Longer:** Natural acids from wild fermentation slow mold and retain moisture, which is why a high-hydration loaf like our Country Sourdough (78% hydration) usually stays pleasant for 4 to 5 days. - **Crust Moisture:** Crust softens as moisture migrates outward. That is normal, and it can be reversed (see Section 3)."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-003",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Can I freeze sourdough, and for how long?",
    "answer": "Yes. Slice the loaf, put parchment between slices, and seal it in a freezer bag at 0\u00b0F (-18\u00b0C). It keeps well for up to 3 months. Toast slices straight from frozen.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "- **Days 1–2:** Place the loaf cut-side down directly on a wooden cutting board at room temperature. The cut face protects the crumb and the crust stays crisp. - **Days 3–5:** Transfer the loaf into an unbleached linen bread bag or brown paper sack. Avoid airtight plastic bags, which soften the crust and encourage mold. - **Location:** Keep away from the stove, direct sun, and humid spots. Ideal room temperature is 65°F–72°F (18°C–22°C)."}]

[{"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "- **Days 1–2:** Place the loaf cut-side down directly on a wooden cutting board at room temperature. The cut face protects the crumb and the crust stays crisp. - **Days 3–5:** Transfer the loaf into an unbleached linen bread bag or brown paper sack. Avoid airtight plastic bags, which soften the crust and encourage mold. - **Location:** Keep away from the stove, direct sun, and humid spots. Ideal room temperature is 65°F–72°F (18°C–22°C)."}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Commercial bread uses mono- and diglycerides and chemical preservatives. Cinder & Salt sourdough contains only flour, water, and sea salt. - **Starch Retrogradation:** Staling is not simply drying out. When bread cools after baking, the gelatinized starch molecules gradually recrystallize and push moisture out of the crumb, which makes it firm and leathery. - **The Refrigerator Trap:** Refrigeration temperatures (35°F–42°F / 1°C–5°C) **accelerate retrogradation by up to 300%**. *Never store fresh artisan bread in the refrigerator.* - **Why Sourdough Lasts Longer:** Natural acids from wild fermentation slow mold and retain moisture, which is why a high-hydration loaf like our Country Sourdough (78% hydration) usually stays pleasant for 4 to 5 days. - **Crust Moisture:** Crust softens as moisture migrates outward. That is normal, and it can be reversed (see Section 3)."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-001",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Should I keep my sourdough loaf in the refrigerator?",
    "answer": "Never refrigerate fresh artisan bread. Refrigerator temperatures (35\u00b0F-42\u00b0F / 1\u00b0C-5\u00b0C) accelerate starch retrogradation by up to 300%, causing the bread to dry out and go stale much faster. Store it cut-side down on a wooden board for days 1-2, then in a linen or paper bag for days 3-5.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-003",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Can I freeze sourdough, and for how long?",
    "answer": "Yes. Slice the loaf, put parchment between slices, and seal it in a freezer bag at 0\u00b0F (-18\u00b0C). It keeps well for up to 3 months. Toast slices straight from frozen.",
