[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-002",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "How do I revive a stale loaf?",
    "answer": "Preheat your oven to 375\u00b0F (190\u00b0C). Run the unsliced loaf under cold water for 3 to 5 seconds, or spritz it well, then bake it directly on the center rack for 6 to 8 minutes. Rest for 5 minutes before slicing. It works best on loaves up to about 3 days old.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "1. **Preheat Oven:** Set the oven to **375°F (190°C)**. 2. **Moisten the Crust:** Run the entire unsliced stale loaf under cold tap water for 3 to 5 seconds, or spritz generously with a water bottle. Do not soak. 3. **Bake:** Place the wet loaf directly on the center oven rack for **6 to 8 minutes**. 4. **Rest:** Allow the loaf to rest for 5 minutes before slicing. Steam gelatinizes the interior crumb, which reverses some of the retrogradation, while the crust crisps. **Tips** - Use this for loaves up to about 3 days old. Beyond that the bread is best sliced and toasted, or made into croutons, breadcrumbs, or panzanella. - Half loaves: cut-side down, 4 to 5 minutes. - Revived bread is best eaten the same day, because it will stale faster the second time. - **Mold:** If you see any mold, discard the whole loaf. Do not cut around it."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-001",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Should I keep my sourdough loaf in the refrigerator?",
    "answer": "Never refrigerate fresh artisan bread. Refrigerator temperatures (35\u00b0F-42\u00b0F / 1\u00b0C-5\u00b0C) accelerate starch retrogradation by up to 300%, causing the bread to dry out and go stale much faster. Store it cut-side down on a wooden board for days 1-2, then in a linen or paper bag for days 3-5.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "1. **Slice first:** Slice the whole loaf on the day you buy it, or the day after. 2. **Separate:** Place parchment squares between slices so you can take out only what you need. 3. **Seal:** Place in a sealed freezer bag and press out as much air as possible. 4. **Freeze:** Store at 0°F (-18°C), and label with the date. 5. **Use:** Toast slices straight from frozen at medium heat. For a whole frozen loaf, thaw on the counter for 2 to 3 hours, then use the refresh technique below. *Note: Enriched pastries (croissants, Danishes, morning buns) are best eaten the same day. Freeze them within 24 hours and reheat at 350°F (177°C) for 8 to 10 minutes.*"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-001",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "How far in advance do I need to book a wedding cake or dessert table?",
    "answer": "We require a minimum of 6 weeks notice for wedding orders and large event catering (100+ guests), 14 days for smaller custom celebratory cakes, and 7 days for small wholesale event trays. Peak wedding season dates (May-October) can close up to 6 months ahead, so early booking is recommended.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Delivery Fee:** $35.00 within Zone A (Urban Core) and $65.00 within Zone B (Outer Metro). Locations beyond our zones are quoted individually. - **Delivery Window:** We deliver and set up during a 2-hour window agreed in your contract, finishing at least 90 minutes before service. - **Venue Requirements:** A level table, shade, and a clear loading path. A refrigerated space within the venue is strongly recommended for tiered cakes. - **Tiered Cakes:** Cakes of three or more tiers are assembled on site by a staff member, and an on-site fee of $100.00 applies. - **Equipment:** Stands, boards, and linens are provided on loan with a refundable $75.00 deposit. They must be returned within 5 days. - **Closed Mondays:** Delivery and pickup are not available on Mondays."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-CAT-004",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "Can my buttercream cake be displayed outdoors in summer?",
    "answer": "Swiss meringue buttercream softens at 72\u00b0F (22\u00b0C) and collapses above 80\u00b0F (27\u00b0C). Above 72\u00b0F (22\u00b0C), outdoor display is limited to 2 hours maximum, and the cake must stay in complete shade because direct sunlight can cause collapse within 15 minutes. For hot events, ask us about a naked or fondant-finished cake.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Weddings & Large Events (100+ guests):** Minimum **6 weeks** notice required. Peak-season dates (May–October) close up to 6 months in advance. - **Bespoke Celebratory Cakes:** Minimum **14 days** notice required. - **Small Wholesale Event Trays:** Minimum **7 days** notice required. - **Rush Requests:** We cannot accommodate bookings inside these windows. Our sourdough cake layers and laminated doughs need 18–36 hours of fermentation, and decorating takes more time on top of that."}]

