[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-002",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Which major allergens are present in your facility?",
    "answer": "Wheat/gluten, rye, tree nuts (walnuts and almonds), sesame, dairy (butter, milk, cheese), and eggs are all handled in our facility. Nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs use color-coded dedicated tools, but ovens, mixers, and the mill are shared, so cross-contact is possible. We do not guarantee any item is free of these allergens.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "We cannot guarantee any event item is free of wheat, tree nuts, sesame, dairy, or eggs. Please share all guest allergies in your intake form. We will give you full ingredient lists and cross-contact notes so you can plan, and we will tell you plainly if an item is not appropriate for a guest. See `ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md`."}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Our products contain wheat or rye and may contain traces of tree nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs. Our facility is **not celiac-safe**. See `ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md`."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-004",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Do you have a nut-free or sesame-free bread?",
    "answer": "Several breads contain no nut or sesame ingredients, such as the Country Sourdough and Spelt Boule. We bake plain loaves first, at 2:00 AM, before seeded and nut items at 4:00 AM, and we sanitize at 12:00 PM and 8:00 PM. Shared ovens and airborne particles mean we cannot guarantee a nut-free or sesame-free product, so people with severe allergies should not rely on us.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-001",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Should I keep my sourdough loaf in the refrigerator?",
    "answer": "Never refrigerate fresh artisan bread. Refrigerator temperatures (35\u00b0F-42\u00b0F / 1\u00b0C-5\u00b0C) accelerate starch retrogradation by up to 300%, causing the bread to dry out and go stale much faster. Store it cut-side down on a wooden board for days 1-2, then in a linen or paper bag for days 3-5.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-003",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Can I freeze sourdough, and for how long?",
    "answer": "Yes. Slice the loaf, put parchment between slices, and seal it in a freezer bag at 0\u00b0F (-18\u00b0C). It keeps well for up to 3 months. Toast slices straight from frozen.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Access Requirements:** Partners must supply a reliable keycode or lockbox key for entry. - **Drop Location:** Drops are made directly to the partner's designated walk-in cooler or food-safe staging counter. Fresh bread is placed in breathable paper or cloth bags, never sealed plastic. - **Cold Weather Protection:** From Nov 1 to Apr 1, exterior lockbox drops are strictly prohibited to prevent thermal shock. A staffed or interior access point is required. - **Refrigeration Warning:** Do not refrigerate bread. Cold storage speeds staling (see `BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md`)."}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Commercial bread uses mono- and diglycerides and chemical preservatives. Cinder & Salt sourdough contains only flour, water, and sea salt. - **Starch Retrogradation:** Staling is not simply drying out. When bread cools after baking, the gelatinized starch molecules gradually recrystallize and push moisture out of the crumb, which makes it firm and leathery. - **The Refrigerator Trap:** Refrigeration temperatures (35°F–42°F / 1°C–5°C) **accelerate retrogradation by up to 300%**. *Never store fresh artisan bread in the refrigerator.* - **Why Sourdough Lasts Longer:** Natural acids from wild fermentation slow mold and retain moisture, which is why a high-hydration loaf like our Country Sourdough (78% hydration) usually stays pleasant for 4 to 5 days. - **Crust Moisture:** Crust softens as moisture migrates outward. That is normal, and it can be reversed (see Section 3)."}]

