[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-001",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Should I keep my sourdough loaf in the refrigerator?",
    "answer": "Never refrigerate fresh artisan bread. Refrigerator temperatures (35\u00b0F-42\u00b0F / 1\u00b0C-5\u00b0C) accelerate starch retrogradation by up to 300%, causing the bread to dry out and go stale much faster. Store it cut-side down on a wooden board for days 1-2, then in a linen or paper bag for days 3-5.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-003",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Can I freeze sourdough, and for how long?",
    "answer": "Yes. Slice the loaf, put parchment between slices, and seal it in a freezer bag at 0\u00b0F (-18\u00b0C). It keeps well for up to 3 months. Toast slices straight from frozen.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "- **Days 1–2:** Place the loaf cut-side down directly on a wooden cutting board at room temperature. The cut face protects the crumb and the crust stays crisp. - **Days 3–5:** Transfer the loaf into an unbleached linen bread bag or brown paper sack. Avoid airtight plastic bags, which soften the crust and encourage mold. - **Location:** Keep away from the stove, direct sun, and humid spots. Ideal room temperature is 65°F–72°F (18°C–22°C)."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-003",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Can I freeze sourdough, and for how long?",
    "answer": "Yes. Slice the loaf, put parchment between slices, and seal it in a freezer bag at 0\u00b0F (-18\u00b0C). It keeps well for up to 3 months. Toast slices straight from frozen.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-002",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "How do I revive a stale loaf?",
    "answer": "Preheat your oven to 375\u00b0F (190\u00b0C). Run the unsliced loaf under cold water for 3 to 5 seconds, or spritz it well, then bake it directly on the center rack for 6 to 8 minutes. Rest for 5 minutes before slicing. It works best on loaves up to about 3 days old.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Commercial bread uses mono- and diglycerides and chemical preservatives. Cinder & Salt sourdough contains only flour, water, and sea salt. - **Starch Retrogradation:** Staling is not simply drying out. When bread cools after baking, the gelatinized starch molecules gradually recrystallize and push moisture out of the crumb, which makes it firm and leathery. - **The Refrigerator Trap:** Refrigeration temperatures (35°F–42°F / 1°C–5°C) **accelerate retrogradation by up to 300%**. *Never store fresh artisan bread in the refrigerator.* - **Why Sourdough Lasts Longer:** Natural acids from wild fermentation slow mold and retain moisture, which is why a high-hydration loaf like our Country Sourdough (78% hydration) usually stays pleasant for 4 to 5 days. - **Crust Moisture:** Crust softens as moisture migrates outward. That is normal, and it can be reversed (see Section 3)."}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "1. **Preheat Oven:** Set the oven to **375°F (190°C)**. 2. **Moisten the Crust:** Run the entire unsliced stale loaf under cold tap water for 3 to 5 seconds, or spritz generously with a water bottle. Do not soak. 3. **Bake:** Place the wet loaf directly on the center oven rack for **6 to 8 minutes**. 4. **Rest:** Allow the loaf to rest for 5 minutes before slicing. Steam gelatinizes the interior crumb, which reverses some of the retrogradation, while the crust crisps. **Tips** - Use this for loaves up to about 3 days old. Beyond that the bread is best sliced and toasted, or made into croutons, breadcrumbs, or panzanella. - Half loaves: cut-side down, 4 to 5 minutes. - Revived bread is best eaten the same day, because it will stale faster the second time. - **Mold:** If you see any mold, discard the whole loaf. Do not cut around it."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-004",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Do you have a nut-free or sesame-free bread?",
    "answer": "Several breads contain no nut or sesame ingredients, such as the Country Sourdough and Spelt Boule. We bake plain loaves first, at 2:00 AM, before seeded and nut items at 4:00 AM, and we sanitize at 12:00 PM and 8:00 PM. Shared ovens and airborne particles mean we cannot guarantee a nut-free or sesame-free product, so people with severe allergies should not rely on us.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-002",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Which major allergens are present in your facility?",
    "answer": "Wheat/gluten, rye, tree nuts (walnuts and almonds), sesame, dairy (butter, milk, cheese), and eggs are all handled in our facility. Nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs use color-coded dedicated tools, but ovens, mixers, and the mill are shared, so cross-contact is possible. We do not guarantee any item is free of these allergens.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md", "quote": "- Every retail item carries a label with ingredients and a "Contains" statement. - A posted notice at the counter and on the website reads: *"Our facility is not celiac-safe. All items may contain traces of wheat, tree nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs."* - Customers with allergies are never told an item is "safe". They are told what is in it and what is nearby. - Any question we cannot answer from the allergen matrix is passed to the Head Baker, and no guess is offered."}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "We cannot guarantee any event item is free of wheat, tree nuts, sesame, dairy, or eggs. Please share all guest allergies in your intake form. We will give you full ingredient lists and cross-contact notes so you can plan, and we will tell you plainly if an item is not appropriate for a guest. See `ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md`."}]

