    "question": "Can my buttercream cake be displayed outdoors in summer?",
    "answer": "Swiss meringue buttercream softens at 72\u00b0F (22\u00b0C) and collapses above 80\u00b0F (27\u00b0C). Above 72\u00b0F (22\u00b0C), outdoor display is limited to 2 hours maximum, and the cake must stay in complete shade because direct sunlight can cause collapse within 15 minutes. For hot events, ask us about a naked or fondant-finished cake.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-RET-002",
    "category": "Daily Retail & Orders",
    "question": "What days are you open?",
    "answer": "We bake and sell Tuesday through Sunday. We are closed on Mondays, which is our rest and fermentation prep day, and there is no retail, delivery, or pickup on Mondays.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-RET-001",
    "category": "Daily Retail & Orders",
    "question": "What time do you usually sell out of Country Sourdough?",
    "answer": "Our daily counter supply generally sells out between 11:30 AM and 1:00 PM on weekdays, and often before 11:00 AM on weekends. We strongly recommend placing a pre-order through our website 48 hours in advance to guarantee your items.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Our sourdough breads undergo a mandatory 24-to-36-hour cold fermentation cycle. Because we use no commercial yeast or dough accelerants, **all wholesale orders require a strict 48-hour advance notice**. - **Standing Order Freeze:** Standing orders lock at **2:00 PM** two days prior to delivery. - **Order Modifications:** Additions or reductions must be submitted through the B2B Portal before the 2:00 PM cutoff. Late modifications cannot be accommodated, because the dough is already mixed and committed. - **No Same-Day or Next-Day Orders:** We do not offer rush orders at any price. | Order Placed By (2:00 PM) | Fermentation & Proofing Window | Delivery Window | | :--- | :--- | :--- | | **Sunday** | Monday | **Tuesday Morning** | | **Monday** | Tuesday | **Wednesday Morning** | | **Tuesday** | Wednesday | **Thursday Morning** | | **Wednesday** | Thursday | **Friday Morning** | | **Thursday** | Friday | **Saturday Morning** | | **Friday** | Saturday | **Sunday Morning** | *Table Summary:* [1. The 48-Hour Fermentation Lead Time Rule] - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Sunday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Monday | Delivery Window: Tuesday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Monday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Tuesday | Delivery Window: Wednesday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Tuesday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Wednesday | Delivery Window: Thursday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Wednesday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Thursday | Delivery Window: Friday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Thursday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Friday | Delivery Window: Saturday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Friday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Saturday | Delivery Window: Sunday Morning *Note: Retail counter and delivery routes are closed on Mondays. Orders placed on Saturday before 2:00 PM are treated as placed for Monday and are not deliverable. They roll to the Sunday cutoff and deliver Tuesday.*"}]

[{"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "1. **Preheat Oven:** Set the oven to **375°F (190°C)**. 2. **Moisten the Crust:** Run the entire unsliced stale loaf under cold tap water for 3 to 5 seconds, or spritz generously with a water bottle. Do not soak. 3. **Bake:** Place the wet loaf directly on the center oven rack for **6 to 8 minutes**. 4. **Rest:** Allow the loaf to rest for 5 minutes before slicing. Steam gelatinizes the interior crumb, which reverses some of the retrogradation, while the crust crisps. **Tips** - Use this for loaves up to about 3 days old. Beyond that the bread is best sliced and toasted, or made into croutons, breadcrumbs, or panzanella. - Half loaves: cut-side down, 4 to 5 minutes. - Revived bread is best eaten the same day, because it will stale faster the second time. - **Mold:** If you see any mold, discard the whole loaf. Do not cut around it."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-002",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "How do I revive a stale loaf?",
    "answer": "Preheat your oven to 375\u00b0F (190\u00b0C). Run the unsliced loaf under cold water for 3 to 5 seconds, or spritz it well, then bake it directly on the center rack for 6 to 8 minutes. Rest for 5 minutes before slicing. It works best on loaves up to about 3 days old.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "- **Days 1–2:** Place the loaf cut-side down directly on a wooden cutting board at room temperature. The cut face protects the crumb and the crust stays crisp. - **Days 3–5:** Transfer the loaf into an unbleached linen bread bag or brown paper sack. Avoid airtight plastic bags, which soften the crust and encourage mold. - **Location:** Keep away from the stove, direct sun, and humid spots. Ideal room temperature is 65°F–72°F (18°C–22°C)."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-001",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Should I keep my sourdough loaf in the refrigerator?",
    "answer": "Never refrigerate fresh artisan bread. Refrigerator temperatures (35\u00b0F-42\u00b0F / 1\u00b0C-5\u00b0C) accelerate starch retrogradation by up to 300%, causing the bread to dry out and go stale much faster. Store it cut-side down on a wooden board for days 1-2, then in a linen or paper bag for days 3-5.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-002",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Which major allergens are present in your facility?",
    "answer": "Wheat/gluten, rye, tree nuts (walnuts and almonds), sesame, dairy (butter, milk, cheese), and eggs are all handled in our facility. Nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs use color-coded dedicated tools, but ovens, mixers, and the mill are shared, so cross-contact is possible. We do not guarantee any item is free of these allergens.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-004",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Do you have a nut-free or sesame-free bread?",
    "answer": "Several breads contain no nut or sesame ingredients, such as the Country Sourdough and Spelt Boule. We bake plain loaves first, at 2:00 AM, before seeded and nut items at 4:00 AM, and we sanitize at 12:00 PM and 8:00 PM. Shared ovens and airborne particles mean we cannot guarantee a nut-free or sesame-free product, so people with severe allergies should not rely on us.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "**Cinder & Salt Bakery** — *Wild Yeast. Heritage Grain. Honest Craft.* Welcome, chefs, café owners, and grocers. This guide explains how we work together. Our bread takes time, so our schedule is firm."}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "We cannot guarantee any event item is free of wheat, tree nuts, sesame, dairy, or eggs. Please share all guest allergies in your intake form. We will give you full ingredient lists and cross-contact notes so you can plan, and we will tell you plainly if an item is not appropriate for a guest. See `ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md`."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-002",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Which major allergens are present in your facility?",
    "answer": "Wheat/gluten, rye, tree nuts (walnuts and almonds), sesame, dairy (butter, milk, cheese), and eggs are all handled in our facility. Nuts, sesame, dairy, and eggs use color-coded dedicated tools, but ovens, mixers, and the mill are shared, so cross-contact is possible. We do not guarantee any item is free of these allergens.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ALG-004",
    "category": "Allergens & Ingredients",
    "question": "Do you have a nut-free or sesame-free bread?",
    "answer": "Several breads contain no nut or sesame ingredients, such as the Country Sourdough and Spelt Boule. We bake plain loaves first, at 2:00 AM, before seeded and nut items at 4:00 AM, and we sanitize at 12:00 PM and 8:00 PM. Shared ovens and airborne particles mean we cannot guarantee a nut-free or sesame-free product, so people with severe allergies should not rely on us.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "ALLERGEN_SAFETY_PROTOCOL.md", "quote": "Cinder & Salt Bakery operates an open-concept bakery and an active stone mill. We mill whole grains (wheat, rye, spelt, einkorn, Red Fife) on-site every week. - **Gluten Presence:** Fine flour particles remain airborne throughout the facility 24 hours a day. No wall, bench, or schedule fully removes them. - **Celiac Disclaimer:** While some items are prepared without gluten-containing ingredients, **our facility is NOT celiac-safe**. We do not produce certified gluten-free products. Customers with Celiac Disease, severe gluten intolerance, or wheat allergy should not consume our products. - **Fermentation Is Not a Safety Claim:** Our 24–36 hour cold fermentation breaks down some starches and phytic acid and makes bread gentler for many people. It does **not** reduce gluten to a celiac-safe level (the regulatory threshold is under 20 ppm). We never market it as doing so. - **Staff Script:** "We're an active stone mill, so flour is in the air all day. We can't make a celiac-safe promise, and we'd rather tell you that honestly than risk your health.""}]

