    "id": "FAQ-CAT-003",
    "category": "Catering & Events",
    "question": "What are your deposit and cancellation terms for events?",
    "answer": "A 50% non-refundable deposit is due at contract signing, and the balance is due 14 days before the event. If you cancel 30 or more days ahead we keep the deposit. If you cancel 14-29 days ahead we retain 50% of the contract value, and under 14 days we retain 100%.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "CATERING_AND_CUSTOM_CAKES.md", "quote": "- **Delivery Fee:** $35.00 within Zone A (Urban Core) and $65.00 within Zone B (Outer Metro). Locations beyond our zones are quoted individually. - **Delivery Window:** We deliver and set up during a 2-hour window agreed in your contract, finishing at least 90 minutes before service. - **Venue Requirements:** A level table, shade, and a clear loading path. A refrigerated space within the venue is strongly recommended for tiered cakes. - **Tiered Cakes:** Cakes of three or more tiers are assembled on site by a staff member, and an on-site fee of $100.00 applies. - **Equipment:** Stands, boards, and linens are provided on loan with a refundable $75.00 deposit. They must be returned within 5 days. - **Closed Mondays:** Delivery and pickup are not available on Mondays."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-WHO-006",
    "category": "Wholesale Accounts",
    "question": "A loaf in my delivery was damaged. How do I file a claim?",
    "answer": "Report missing, damaged, or unsatisfactory items within 12 hours of the delivery drop, and include cross-section photos. Approved claims are credited to your next invoice. Claims made after 12 hours cannot be accepted.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-WHO-001",
    "category": "Wholesale Accounts",
    "question": "Can I modify my wholesale delivery order for tomorrow morning?",
    "answer": "All wholesale orders must adhere to our 48-hour fermentation lead time. Standing order modifications for tomorrow morning locked today at 2:00 PM. Any changes requested past 2:00 PM cannot be processed as our doughs are already in their final fermentation cycle.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_ORDERING_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Our sourdough breads undergo a mandatory 24-to-36-hour cold fermentation cycle. Because we use no commercial yeast or dough accelerants, **all wholesale orders require a strict 48-hour advance notice**. - **Standing Order Freeze:** Standing orders lock at **2:00 PM** two days prior to delivery. - **Order Modifications:** Additions or reductions must be submitted through the B2B Portal before the 2:00 PM cutoff. Late modifications cannot be accommodated, because the dough is already mixed and committed. - **No Same-Day or Next-Day Orders:** We do not offer rush orders at any price. | Order Placed By (2:00 PM) | Fermentation & Proofing Window | Delivery Window | | :--- | :--- | :--- | | **Sunday** | Monday | **Tuesday Morning** | | **Monday** | Tuesday | **Wednesday Morning** | | **Tuesday** | Wednesday | **Thursday Morning** | | **Wednesday** | Thursday | **Friday Morning** | | **Thursday** | Friday | **Saturday Morning** | | **Friday** | Saturday | **Sunday Morning** | *Table Summary:* [1. The 48-Hour Fermentation Lead Time Rule] - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Sunday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Monday | Delivery Window: Tuesday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Monday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Tuesday | Delivery Window: Wednesday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Tuesday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Wednesday | Delivery Window: Thursday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Wednesday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Thursday | Delivery Window: Friday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Thursday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Friday | Delivery Window: Saturday Morning - Order Placed By (2:00 PM): Friday | Fermentation & Proofing Window: Saturday | Delivery Window: Sunday Morning *Note: Retail counter and delivery routes are closed on Mondays. Orders placed on Saturday before 2:00 PM are treated as placed for Monday and are not deliverable. They roll to the Sunday cutoff and deliver Tuesday.*"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-001",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Should I keep my sourdough loaf in the refrigerator?",
    "answer": "Never refrigerate fresh artisan bread. Refrigerator temperatures (35\u00b0F-42\u00b0F / 1\u00b0C-5\u00b0C) accelerate starch retrogradation by up to 300%, causing the bread to dry out and go stale much faster. Store it cut-side down on a wooden board for days 1-2, then in a linen or paper bag for days 3-5.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "Commercial bread uses mono- and diglycerides and chemical preservatives. Cinder & Salt sourdough contains only flour, water, and sea salt. - **Starch Retrogradation:** Staling is not simply drying out. When bread cools after baking, the gelatinized starch molecules gradually recrystallize and push moisture out of the crumb, which makes it firm and leathery. - **The Refrigerator Trap:** Refrigeration temperatures (35°F–42°F / 1°C–5°C) **accelerate retrogradation by up to 300%**. *Never store fresh artisan bread in the refrigerator.* - **Why Sourdough Lasts Longer:** Natural acids from wild fermentation slow mold and retain moisture, which is why a high-hydration loaf like our Country Sourdough (78% hydration) usually stays pleasant for 4 to 5 days. - **Crust Moisture:** Crust softens as moisture migrates outward. That is normal, and it can be reversed (see Section 3)."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-STR-003",
    "category": "Bread Care & Storage",
    "question": "Can I freeze sourdough, and for how long?",
    "answer": "Yes. Slice the loaf, put parchment between slices, and seal it in a freezer bag at 0\u00b0F (-18\u00b0C). It keeps well for up to 3 months. Toast slices straight from frozen.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "BREAD_STORAGE_AND_REVIVAL.md", "quote": "- **Days 1–2:** Place the loaf cut-side down directly on a wooden cutting board at room temperature. The cut face protects the crumb and the crust stays crisp. - **Days 3–5:** Transfer the loaf into an unbleached linen bread bag or brown paper sack. Avoid airtight plastic bags, which soften the crust and encourage mold. - **Location:** Keep away from the stove, direct sun, and humid spots. Ideal room temperature is 65°F–72°F (18°C–22°C)."}]

