    "id": "FAQ-WHO-001",
    "category": "Wholesale & B2B",
    "question": "Can we deboss our company logo onto wholesale leather field notebooks?",
    "answer": "Yes. We offer custom hot-stamp debossing on orders meeting our Tier 1 MOQ of 25 units. A one-time $150 brass die tooling fee applies, and artwork must be supplied as a vectorized PDF or AI file.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WHOLESALE_SOP.md", "quote": "Hot-stamp custom logo debossing is available across all leather goods. Co-branded apparel patches are available for Tier 2 and 3 accounts. - **Artwork:** Vector files (.AI or .PDF) are required. Raster files (JPG/PNG) are not accepted. - **Tool-and-Die Fee:** A one-time **$150 tool-and-die brass mold fee** applies per custom artwork submission. Reorders using the same die are free. - **Size Limits:** Maximum deboss area is **2.5 in. × 1.25 in.** on belts and **3 in. × 3 in.** on notebooks and wallets. Minimum line weight is 0.5 pt (1 mm gap between elements). - **Stamp Temperature:** Stamps are applied at roughly **230°F–260°F (110°C–127°C)**. - **Proof Approval:** A digital proof is sent within 3 business days of artwork receipt, and a physical sample deboss is available for $35.00. Production starts only after written proof approval. - **Brand Compliance:** We reserve the right to decline artwork that infringes a trademark or conflicts with our brand. - Custom branded goods are **final sale**."}, {"source": "RETURNS_AND_EXCHANGES.md", "quote": "The following are non-returnable and excluded from refund policies: - Custom monogrammed leather goods. - Altered sleeve hems and any other custom tailoring. - Factory seconds. - Archived sale batches, labeled "Final Sale" at checkout. - Gift cards. Monogrammed or hemmed items are still covered by the Heritage Guarantee for defects."}]

[{"source": "CARE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- Store gear in **breathable canvas storage bags** in well-ventilated spaces. Avoid plastic bags and sealed containers. - Keep the temperature between 50°F and 75°F (10°C and 24°C) and relative humidity under 60% where possible. - Avoid direct UV sunlight, which fades and dries both canvas and leather, and avoid damp basements, which promote mold growth and leather degradation. - Store coats on a wide hanger. Fold waxed canvas as little as possible, because hard creases can crack the wax. - Before storage, make sure everything is completely dry."}, {"source": "CARE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Biannual Conditioning:** Apply natural beeswax or neatsfoot oil balm every **6 months** to prevent dry cracking. - Dry climates (under 30% relative humidity): condition every **3 to 4 months**. - Humid climates (over 70%): condition every 6 months, and watch for mildew. - **How to Condition:** 1. Brush off dirt and dust. 2. Wipe with a damp cloth and let it dry for 1 hour at room temperature. 3. Work a **dime-sized amount** of balm in with a clean cloth, using small circles. 4. Let it absorb for 12 hours, then buff off the excess. - Don't over-oil. Too much oil softens the leather and makes it stretch. - **Cleaning Protocol:** Wipe with a damp cloth. Use saddle soap sparingly for deep mud or grease removal, and condition afterward. - **Prohibitions:** *Strictly no machine washing or heat drying.* High temperature strips natural oils and distorts the grain. Keep leather away from radiators, wood stoves, and car dashboards. - **If It Gets Soaked:** Blot with a towel, stuff boots or belts with paper if needed, and dry slowly at room temperature, for **24 to 48 hours**, out of direct sun. Condition after it is dry. - **Patina:** Darkening, scratches, and creases are normal. Veg-tan leather ages to a deeper color the more you use it. - **Hardware:** Solid brass tarnishes. A little tarnish is fine. To clean it, use a dry cloth. Avoid chemical polishes on leather."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MAT-003",
    "category": "Product & Materials",
    "question": "How often should I condition my leather?",
    "answer": "Every 6 months with natural beeswax or neatsfoot oil balm. In dry climates, every 3 to 4 months. Use a dime-sized amount, work it in with a clean cloth, wait 12 hours, and buff off the excess. Don't over-oil.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MAT-004",
    "category": "Product & Materials",
    "question": "Can I machine wash my waxed canvas coat?",
    "answer": "No. Machine washing or heat drying strips the wax and ruins the water resistance. Brush off dry mud, spot clean with cold water and a soft brush, and air dry. If you already washed it, you can rewax it.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MAT-005",
    "category": "Product & Materials",
    "question": "How do I rewax my coat?",
    "answer": "Clean the canvas with cold water and a soft brush, then let it dry. Rub a paraffin/beeswax bar over the weave in circular motions, then melt it in with a hairdryer on medium or a heat gun on low, held 4 to 6 inches away. Cure it in a cool, dry place for 24 hours.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-REP-005",
    "category": "Warranty & Repair",
    "question": "My coat is damaged beyond repair. What are my options?",
    "answer": "When structural repair would compromise safety or integrity, you receive a 40% trade-in credit toward a replacement item, valid for 12 months. A bench craftsperson makes the repair-versus-replace call and we explain it with photos.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "CARE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "| Mistake | What Happens | Fix | | :--- | :--- | :--- | | Washing a waxed coat in a machine | Wax strips out, water resistance gone | Rewax (Section 2) | | Drying boots by a fire | Leather shrinks and cracks | Replace; prevention only | | Over-oiling | Leather goes soft and sags | Let it rest and don't oil again for 6 months | | Storing damp | Mold and mildew | Brush off, dry, and see `REPAIR_POLICY.md` for exclusions | *Table Summary:* [5. Common Mistakes] - Mistake: Washing a waxed coat in a machine | What Happens: Wax strips out, water resistance gone | Fix: Rewax (Section 2) - Mistake: Drying boots by a fire | What Happens: Leather shrinks and cracks | Fix: Replace; prevention only - Mistake: Over-oiling | What Happens: Leather goes soft and sags | Fix: Let it rest and don't oil again for 6 months - Mistake: Storing damp | What Happens: Mold and mildew | Fix: Brush off, dry, and see REPAIR_POLICY.md for exclusions Need help? Contact our support team, and we'll walk you through it."}, {"source": "REPAIR_POLICY.md", "quote": "- **Patina is character, not a defect.** Scratches, darkening, creases, and fading are part of the gear's life. - **Neglect:** Gear left wet and balled up for weeks, mold growth, or leather cracked from never being conditioned is not covered. We will still quote you a paid repair. - **Abuse:** Cutting, burning, using a coat as a tow strap, or hitting a belt with an industrial tool is not "regular field use". - **Third-party work:** A tailor re-stitching a seam voids coverage for that seam only. The rest of the item stays covered."}]

