, and we'll walk you through it."}]

[{"source": "CARE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Biannual Conditioning:** Apply natural beeswax or neatsfoot oil balm every **6 months** to prevent dry cracking. - Dry climates (under 30% relative humidity): condition every **3 to 4 months**. - Humid climates (over 70%): condition every 6 months, and watch for mildew. - **How to Condition:** 1. Brush off dirt and dust. 2. Wipe with a damp cloth and let it dry for 1 hour at room temperature. 3. Work a **dime-sized amount** of balm in with a clean cloth, using small circles. 4. Let it absorb for 12 hours, then buff off the excess. - Don't over-oil. Too much oil softens the leather and makes it stretch. - **Cleaning Protocol:** Wipe with a damp cloth. Use saddle soap sparingly for deep mud or grease removal, and condition afterward. - **Prohibitions:** *Strictly no machine washing or heat drying.* High temperature strips natural oils and distorts the grain. Keep leather away from radiators, wood stoves, and car dashboards. - **If It Gets Soaked:** Blot with a towel, stuff boots or belts with paper if needed, and dry slowly at room temperature, for **24 to 48 hours**, out of direct sun. Condition after it is dry. - **Patina:** Darkening, scratches, and creases are normal. Veg-tan leather ages to a deeper color the more you use it. - **Hardware:** Solid brass tarnishes. A little tarnish is fine. To clean it, use a dry cloth. Avoid chemical polishes on leather."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MAT-003",
    "category": "Product & Materials",
    "question": "How often should I condition my leather?",
    "answer": "Every 6 months with natural beeswax or neatsfoot oil balm. In dry climates, every 3 to 4 months. Use a dime-sized amount, work it in with a clean cloth, wait 12 hours, and buff off the excess. Don't over-oil.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MAT-006",
    "category": "Product & Materials",
    "question": "My leather got soaked. What do I do?",
    "answer": "Blot it with a towel and dry it slowly at room temperature for 24 to 48 hours, away from heat and direct sun. Never use a radiator, stove, or hair dryer. Once dry, condition it. Heat-damaged leather cracks and is not covered by the guarantee.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CARE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- Brush off dry mud and dust with a soft brush. - Spot clean with **cold water** and a soft brush or sponge. Do not use soap, detergent, or solvents, because they strip the wax. - Hang to air dry, away from direct heat. - **Never** machine wash, dry clean, or tumble dry."}]

