[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MAT-004",
    "category": "Product & Materials",
    "question": "Can I machine wash my waxed canvas coat?",
    "answer": "No. Machine washing or heat drying strips the wax and ruins the water resistance. Brush off dry mud, spot clean with cold water and a soft brush, and air dry. If you already washed it, you can rewax it.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MAT-005",
    "category": "Product & Materials",
    "question": "How do I rewax my coat?",
    "answer": "Clean the canvas with cold water and a soft brush, then let it dry. Rub a paraffin/beeswax bar over the weave in circular motions, then melt it in with a hairdryer on medium or a heat gun on low, held 4 to 6 inches away. Cure it in a cool, dry place for 24 hours.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CARE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Rewax once a year, or seasonally for heavy use, or whenever water stops beading. 1. **Clean:** Remove debris using cold water and a soft brush. Let the canvas dry completely. 2. **Apply:** Rub a paraffin/beeswax bar evenly across the canvas weave using circular motions. Focus on seams, shoulders, elbows, and pocket flaps. Use about **one 3 oz bar per coat**. 3. **Melt:** Use a heat gun on its low setting or a hairdryer on medium heat, held **4 to 6 inches (10 to 15 cm)** from the fabric, to melt the wax into the fibers. Keep it moving. Do not exceed about **150°F (65°C)** at the fabric surface. 4. **Even Out:** Wipe off any pooling wax with a clean cloth while it's warm. 5. **Cure:** Allow the canvas to cure flat or hanging in a cool, dry area for **24 hours**. Test on a hidden area first. Fresh wax may darken the canvas slightly."}, {"source": "CARE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- Store gear in **breathable canvas storage bags** in well-ventilated spaces. Avoid plastic bags and sealed containers. - Keep the temperature between 50°F and 75°F (10°C and 24°C) and relative humidity under 60% where possible. - Avoid direct UV sunlight, which fades and dries both canvas and leather, and avoid damp basements, which promote mold growth and leather degradation. - Store coats on a wide hanger. Fold waxed canvas as little as possible, because hard creases can crack the wax. - Before storage, make sure everything is completely dry."}]

