    }
}"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ECO-001",
    "category": "Chemical Safety & Eco-Standards",
    "question": "Are your cleaning products safe for homes with infants and pets?",
    "answer": "Our products are zero-VOC, plant- or mineral-derived, and EPA Design for the Environment (DfE) and Green Seal certified. Our hospital-grade disinfectant uses 0.05% thymol, a thyme extract, and kills 99.99% of bacteria and enveloped viruses on a 10-minute wet dwell. Botanical does not mean harmless, so we ask that pets and infants stay out of a room until surfaces dry.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ECO-002",
    "category": "Chemical Safety & Eco-Standards",
    "question": "What ingredients do you use?",
    "answer": "Bio-Shield Disinfectant uses 0.05% thymol, OxyPure uses stabilized 3% hydrogen peroxide, Citrus-Loom Descaler uses 8% food-grade citric acid, and PureLoom Floor Wash is a saponified coconut and olive oil soap. We provide Safety Data Sheets for every product on request.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CHEMICAL_SAFETY_AND_SDS_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Kettle & Loom exclusively deploys EPA Design for the Environment (DfE) and Green Seal certified cleaning chemistry: | Product / Formulation | Active Botanical Base | Target Pathogens / Soils | Dwell Time Required | | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | | **Bio-Shield Hospital Disinfectant** | Thymol (0.05% botanical thyme extract) | 99.99% bacteria, enveloped viruses, fungi (per label claims) | 10 minutes (wet dwell) | | **OxyPure Multi-Surface Cleaner** | Hydrogen Peroxide (stabilized 3%) | Soap scum, organic grease, protein soils | 3 minutes | | **Citrus-Loom Mineral Descaler** | Organic Citric Acid (food-grade 8%) | Hard water scale, calcium deposits, rust | 5 minutes | | **PureLoom Floor Wash** | Saponified Coconut & Olive Oil Soap | Hardwood, engineered plank, stone tiles | Immediate mop dry | *Table Summary:* [1. Approved Botanical Formulations & Active Ingredients] - Product / Formulation: Bio-Shield Hospital Disinfectant | Active Botanical Base: Thymol (0.05% botanical thyme extract) | Target Pathogens / Soils: 99.99% bacteria, enveloped viruses, fungi (per label claims) | Dwell Time Required: 10 minutes (wet dwell) - Product / Formulation: OxyPure Multi-Surface Cleaner | Active Botanical Base: Hydrogen Peroxide (stabilized 3%) | Target Pathogens / Soils: Soap scum, organic grease, protein soils | Dwell Time Required: 3 minutes - Product / Formulation: Citrus-Loom Mineral Descaler | Active Botanical Base: Organic Citric Acid (food-grade 8%) | Target Pathogens / Soils: Hard water scale, calcium deposits, rust | Dwell Time Required: 5 minutes - Product / Formulation: PureLoom Floor Wash | Active Botanical Base: Saponified Coconut & Olive Oil Soap | Target Pathogens / Soils: Hardwood, engineered plank, stone tiles | Dwell Time Required: Immediate mop dry"}]

[{"source": "CHEMICAL_SAFETY_AND_SDS_GUIDE.md", "quote": "* **Strict Acid Prohibition:** *Citrus-Loom Descaler and acidic solutions must NEVER be applied to unsealed natural marble, travertine, limestone, or terrazzo.* Acid etching permanently damages calcite stone. * **Hardwood Neutrality:** Wood flooring must be cleaned exclusively with pH-neutral PureLoom wash. Never saturate it with standing water. * **Granite & Quartz:** Use OxyPure only. No descaler on sealed stone unless the sealer is confirmed intact and the surface is rinsed within 5 minutes. * **Oil-Rubbed Bronze & Unlacquered Brass:** Dry microfiber only. No acids or peroxide, as they strip the finish. * **Unsealed Butcher Block:** Damp wipe with PureLoom diluted to the label ratio. Do not apply disinfectant to unsealed wood for a full dwell time. * **Wool Rugs & Natural Fibers:** Do not apply hydrogen peroxide, because it may bleach dyed fibers. Spot test first in a hidden area. Always check the client's Surface Sensitivity Map (see `WALKTHROUGH_AND_INTAKE_SOP.md`) before opening a product."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-SCP-001",
    "category": "Scope of Services",
    "question": "Do you clean unsealed marble or antique wood furniture?",
    "answer": "During your initial walkthrough, our team maps all sensitive surfaces. We use pH-neutral saponified soap washes for hardwood and unsealed stone. We strictly prohibit the use of acidic descalers on marble, limestone, or terrazzo to prevent etching.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-SCP-004",
    "category": "Scope of Services",
    "question": "Is there anything you won't clean?",
    "answer": "We do not handle biohazards, mold remediation, or asbestos and lead abatement, and we don't move furniture over 50 lb. We also do not use acid on unsealed natural stone.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "CHEMICAL_SAFETY_AND_SDS_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Never mix** products with one another. Never combine hydrogen peroxide with vinegar or other acids, because they form peracetic acid, an irritant. - **Never** mix with chlorine bleach or ammonia brought by a client. - Dilute concentrates only with the metered dispensers, using room-temperature water (60°F–80°F / 16°C–27°C). Hot water above 120°F (49°C) degrades hydrogen peroxide and thymol. - Use fresh dilutions. Discard unused mixed solutions after **7 days**."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ECO-005",
    "category": "Chemical Safety & Eco-Standards",
    "question": "A cleaning product got in my eye. What should I do?",
    "answer": "Flush your eye with clean water for 10 minutes with the eyelid held open, remove contact lenses if you can, and seek medical attention if irritation persists. For ingestion, rinse the mouth and call Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222. Then contact us so we can provide the SDS and file an incident report.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "CHEMICAL_SAFETY_AND_SDS_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Every product has a Safety Data Sheet (SDS) in the technician binder and the mobile app, readable offline. | Exposure | Response | | :--- | :--- | | **Inhalation** | Low-hazard, zero-VOC formulations. Move to fresh air if anyone has irritation and ventilate the room. Seek medical attention if symptoms last more than 15 minutes. | | **Eye Contact** | In case of direct splash, flush eyes with potable water for **10 minutes**, holding the eyelids open. Remove contact lenses if easy to do. Seek medical attention if irritation continues. | | **Skin Contact** | Rinse with water for **5 minutes**. Remove contaminated clothing. Seek medical advice if redness persists. | | **Ingestion** | Rinse mouth. Do not induce vomiting. Call Poison Control at **1-800-222-1222** (U.S.) and follow their directions. | *Table Summary:* [3. SDS Exposure Protocols & First Aid] - Exposure: Inhalation | Response: Low-hazard, zero-VOC formulations. Move to fresh air if anyone has irritation and ventilate the room. Seek medical attention if symptoms last more than 15 minutes. - Exposure: Eye Contact | Response: In case of direct splash, flush eyes with potable water for 10 minutes, holding the eyelids open. Remove contact lenses if easy to do. Seek medical attention if irritation continues. - Exposure: Skin Contact | Response: Rinse with water for 5 minutes. Remove contaminated clothing. Seek medical advice if redness persists. - Exposure: Ingestion | Response: Rinse mouth. Do not induce vomiting. Call Poison Control at 1-800-222-1222 (U.S.) and follow their directions. **Note:** Concentrated 3% hydrogen peroxide and 8% citric acid can irritate eyes and skin. Wear nitrile gloves and eye protection when pouring or diluting concentrates."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ACC-005",
    "category": "Property Access & Security",
    "question": "Something in my home was damaged during a cleaning. What now?",
    "answer": "Report it within 24 hours with photos. We maintain liability insurance and bonding, and claims are reviewed within 5 business days. We will compare against the pre-existing condition photos taken at your walkthrough.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-PST-001",
    "category": "Post-Construction Services",
    "question": "Why does post-construction cleaning require multiple passes?",
    "answer": "Drywall and sawdust particles remain suspended in the air for up to 48 hours after construction wraps up. We utilize certified 0.3-micron HEPA vacuums and a structured three-phase process (rough pass, deep vacuum, damp microfiber sparkle wipe) to prevent settling dust from coating surfaces after cleaning.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-PST-005",
    "category": "Post-Construction Services",
    "question": "Dust came back a few days after you cleaned. Can you return?",
    "answer": "Fine dust can resettle for several days after finishing. We offer a touch-up visit within 7 days of the final clean, and will walk the punch list with you or your contractor within 48 hours.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "POST_CONSTRUCTION_REMEDIATION_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Post-construction cleaning requires a sequential three-phase approach to capture settling airborne drywall gypsum and sawdust: 1. **Phase 1: Rough Debris Pass:** Removal of construction packaging, protective films, tape residue, and rough floor sweeping with HEPA capture. 2. **Phase 2: High-to-Low HEPA Vacuuming:** Dry particle extraction starting from ceiling joists, HVAC diffusers, door casings, down to baseboards. 3. **Phase 3: Wet Microfiber Sparkle Pass:** Multi-bucket damp wipe using electrostatic microfiber cloths to capture remaining microscopic dust films."}, {"source": "service_scope_matrix.json", "quote": "Moving heavy furniture (over 50 lb)"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ACC-002",
    "category": "Property Access & Security",
    "question": "How do you handle my keys and door codes?",
    "answer": "A smart lock with a unique service PIN is preferred. If you use physical keys, they stay in an on-site lockbox, and technicians never carry permanent property keys off-site. Alarm and access codes are stored in encrypted profiles.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ACC-001",
    "category": "Property Access & Security",
    "question": "Do I need to be home when the cleaning collective arrives?",
    "answer": "No. Most residential and commercial clients provide a smart lock code or lockbox key. Our technicians arrive within the designated arrival window, complete the service scope, re-secure all windows and doors, and set the security alarm upon departure.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "PROPERTY_ACCESS_AND_CANCELLATION_POLICY.md", "quote": "* **Digital Keypads & Smart Locks:** Preferred access method. Clients provide a unique service PIN designated exclusively for Kettle & Loom staff. We recommend a PIN that is active only during the service window. * **Physical Lockboxes:** If using physical keys, keys must remain inside a secure on-site lockbox. Technicians will not carry permanent property keys off-site. Lockbox codes are shared only with the assigned team and changed on request. * **Alarm Systems:** Alarm disarm codes and re-arming instructions must be stored in encrypted client profiles. Any false alarm dispatch fees resulting from inaccurate client codes are the client's responsibility. - Technicians disarm on arrival and re-arm on departure. If an alarm triggers, the technician calls the client and the alarm company within **5 minutes** and gives the correct passcode. - Provide your alarm company's name and the verbal passcode for dispatch. * **Departure Procedure:** Technicians re-lock all doors, close windows, set the thermostat to its original setting, turn off lights, and text a completion notice. * **Key Custody:** Access details are visible only to the Operations team and the assigned technicians. Access codes are removed from the technician's device when the contract ends. * **Garage and Gate Codes:** Provide any gate, garage, or elevator access in your profile."}, {"source": "PROPERTY_ACCESS_AND_CANCELLATION_POLICY.md", "quote": "A lockout occurs when technicians cannot enter the property within **20 minutes** of the start of the arrival window because of an incorrect code, a missing key, a deactivated PIN, or a locked interior door. - The technician tries the client's contact numbers and waits up to 20 minutes. - If entry is still not possible, the visit is recorded as a lockout and billed at **100% of the scheduled service fee**. - A one-time free rebooking is not offered for lockouts, but we are glad to help get the next visit set up. - Lockouts due to a building-management error are reviewed on a case-by-case basis."}]

