}"}, {"source": "POST_CONSTRUCTION_REMEDIATION_GUIDE.md", "quote": "| Phase | Typical Labor Share | Key Tasks | Do Not | | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | | 1. Rough Debris | 20% | Remove films, stickers, tape, and packaging; scrape paint splatter with plastic blades; sweep with HEPA-equipped machines or use damp mopping | Dry sweep with a standard broom, which re-suspends dust | | 2. High-to-Low HEPA | 40% | Ceiling fans, light fixtures, vents, tops of cabinets, window tracks, shelves, then walls, then baseboards, then floors | Use a household vacuum without HEPA, or vacuum low areas first | | 3. Wet Microfiber | 40% | Wipe all horizontal and vertical surfaces, fixtures, glass, and cabinet interiors | Use a wet cloth on thick dry dust; vacuum first | *Table Summary:* [Phase Details] - Phase: 1. Rough Debris | Typical Labor Share: 20% | Key Tasks: Remove films, stickers, tape, and packaging; scrape paint splatter with plastic blades; sweep with HEPA-equipped machines or use damp mopping | Do Not: Dry sweep with a standard broom, which re-suspends dust - Phase: 2. High-to-Low HEPA | Typical Labor Share: 40% | Key Tasks: Ceiling fans, light fixtures, vents, tops of cabinets, window tracks, shelves, then walls, then baseboards, then floors | Do Not: Use a household vacuum without HEPA, or vacuum low areas first - Phase: 3. Wet Microfiber | Typical Labor Share: 40% | Key Tasks: Wipe all horizontal and vertical surfaces, fixtures, glass, and cabinet interiors | Do Not: Use a wet cloth on thick dry dust; vacuum first - Work **high to low** and **back to front**, and finish at the entrance. - Allow dust to settle for at least **24 to 48 hours** after the last drywall sanding or cutting before the final pass. We usually schedule Phase 2 and Phase 3 as separate visits. - Typical labor estimates are in `service_scope_matrix.json` (about 240 minutes per 1,000 sq ft for a full three-phase clean)."}]

[{"source": "POST_CONSTRUCTION_REMEDIATION_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **White Glove Test:** A clean white microfiber cloth wiped across a horizontal surface at **3 sample locations per room** should pick up no visible dust. - **Flashlight Inspection:** A raking-light check of painted surfaces and glass at a low angle. - **Walkthrough:** The client or general contractor does a final walkthrough with the Site Lead, with a punch list completed within **48 hours**. - HVAC: Replace the system filter after the final pass. Ducts are not cleaned under this service."}, {"source": "POST_CONSTRUCTION_REMEDIATION_GUIDE.md", "quote": "* **Equipment Standard:** Commercial HEPA vacuums certified to capture **99.97% of particulates down to 0.3 microns**. Certification labels must be on the machine, and filters are inspected before every project. * **Filter Maintenance:** Replace or clean HEPA filters when the indicator shows full, or at least every **40 hours** of use. Empty bags and canisters outdoors or into a sealed bag at the end of every shift, away from the work area. * **Air Scrubbers:** For projects with heavy dust, place a HEPA air scrubber (rated at 4+ air changes per hour for the room volume) in the room during Phase 2, and keep it running for 4 hours afterward. * **Microfiber Fold Technique:** Microfiber cloths must be folded into **8 clean quadrants** and flipped continuously to prevent spreading dry drywall dust across painted walls. - When all 8 quadrants are soiled, the cloth goes in the used pile. Never dip a used cloth back in the clean solution. - Use the multi-bucket system: one bucket of clean solution, one of rinse, and fresh cloths from a clean stack. - Use **damp**, not wet, cloths, wrung out so that no water drips. Wet cloths turn drywall dust into a paste. * **Surface Cleaner:** OxyPure, diluted to label, or plain water on delicate finishes. See `CHEMICAL_SAFETY_AND_SDS_GUIDE.md`."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-PST-001",
    "category": "Post-Construction Services",
    "question": "Why does post-construction cleaning require multiple passes?",
    "answer": "Drywall and sawdust particles remain suspended in the air for up to 48 hours after construction wraps up. We utilize certified 0.3-micron HEPA vacuums and a structured three-phase process (rough pass, deep vacuum, damp microfiber sparkle wipe) to prevent settling dust from coating surfaces after cleaning.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-PST-002",
    "category": "Post-Construction Services",
    "question": "What is a HEPA vacuum and why do you use one?",
    "answer": "A certified HEPA vacuum captures 99.97% of particles down to 0.3 microns. Household vacuums blow fine drywall dust back into the air, whereas HEPA machines trap it.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-SCP-003",
    "category": "Scope of Services",
    "question": "Do you clean short-term rentals between guests?",
    "answer": "Yes. Our turnover service takes about 110 minutes per 1,000 sq ft per technician and includes laundering linens with hypoallergenic wash, hotel-standard bed making, restocking amenities, sanitizing high-touch surfaces, and checking for left-behind property, with photos sent when finished.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-PST-005",
    "category": "Post-Construction Services",
    "question": "Dust came back a few days after you cleaned. Can you return?",
    "answer": "Fine dust can resettle for several days after finishing. We offer a touch-up visit within 7 days of the final clean, and will walk the punch list with you or your contractor within 48 hours.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "POST_CONSTRUCTION_REMEDIATION_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Post-construction cleaning requires a sequential three-phase approach to capture settling airborne drywall gypsum and sawdust: 1. **Phase 1: Rough Debris Pass:** Removal of construction packaging, protective films, tape residue, and rough floor sweeping with HEPA capture. 2. **Phase 2: High-to-Low HEPA Vacuuming:** Dry particle extraction starting from ceiling joists, HVAC diffusers, door casings, down to baseboards. 3. **Phase 3: Wet Microfiber Sparkle Pass:** Multi-bucket damp wipe using electrostatic microfiber cloths to capture remaining microscopic dust films."}, {"source": "POST_CONSTRUCTION_REMEDIATION_GUIDE.md", "quote": "| Stage | Timing | | :--- | :--- | | Rough clean (Phase 1) | After trade work, before final painting touch-ups | | Final clean (Phases 2–3) | After all trades are done, no earlier than 24–48 hours after last sanding | | Touch-up visit | Within 7 days of the final clean, if needed | *Table Summary:* [5. Scheduling] - Stage: Rough clean (Phase 1) | Timing: After trade work, before final painting touch-ups - Stage: Final clean (Phases 2–3) | Timing: After all trades are done, no earlier than 24–48 hours after last sanding - Stage: Touch-up visit | Timing: Within 7 days of the final clean, if needed Dust can resettle for several days after finishing, which is why a short touch-up visit is offered."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-SCP-001",
    "category": "Scope of Services",
    "question": "Do you clean unsealed marble or antique wood furniture?",
    "answer": "During your initial walkthrough, our team maps all sensitive surfaces. We use pH-neutral saponified soap washes for hardwood and unsealed stone. We strictly prohibit the use of acidic descalers on marble, limestone, or terrazzo to prevent etching.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "service_scope_matrix.json", "quote": "Moving heavy furniture (over 50 lb)"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-SCP-004",
    "category": "Scope of Services",
    "question": "Is there anything you won't clean?",
    "answer": "We do not handle biohazards, mold remediation, or asbestos and lead abatement, and we don't move furniture over 50 lb. We also do not use acid on unsealed natural stone.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WALKTHROUGH_AND_INTAKE_SOP.md", "quote": "| Item | Recorded | | :--- | :--- | | Square footage by room | Yes | | Floor types by room | Yes | | Sensitive surfaces | Yes | | Pre-existing damage (photo log) | Yes | | Pets, infants, allergies | Yes | | Access method and alarm details | Yes (encrypted) | | Parking and loading | Yes | | Water and power access | Yes | | Client-supplied cleaning products | Note: not used unless approved | *Table Summary:* [3. Walkthrough Checklist] - Item: Square footage by room | Recorded: Yes - Item: Floor types by room | Recorded: Yes - Item: Sensitive surfaces | Recorded: Yes - Item: Pre-existing damage (photo log) | Recorded: Yes - Item: Pets, infants, allergies | Recorded: Yes - Item: Access method and alarm details | Recorded: Yes (encrypted) - Item: Parking and loading | Recorded: Yes - Item: Water and power access | Recorded: Yes - Item: Client-supplied cleaning products | Recorded: Note: not used unless approved"}]

[{"source": "PROPERTY_ACCESS_AND_CANCELLATION_POLICY.md", "quote": "* **Digital Keypads & Smart Locks:** Preferred access method. Clients provide a unique service PIN designated exclusively for Kettle & Loom staff. We recommend a PIN that is active only during the service window. * **Physical Lockboxes:** If using physical keys, keys must remain inside a secure on-site lockbox. Technicians will not carry permanent property keys off-site. Lockbox codes are shared only with the assigned team and changed on request. * **Alarm Systems:** Alarm disarm codes and re-arming instructions must be stored in encrypted client profiles. Any false alarm dispatch fees resulting from inaccurate client codes are the client's responsibility. - Technicians disarm on arrival and re-arm on departure. If an alarm triggers, the technician calls the client and the alarm company within **5 minutes** and gives the correct passcode. - Provide your alarm company's name and the verbal passcode for dispatch. * **Departure Procedure:** Technicians re-lock all doors, close windows, set the thermostat to its original setting, turn off lights, and text a completion notice. * **Key Custody:** Access details are visible only to the Operations team and the assigned technicians. Access codes are removed from the technician's device when the contract ends. * **Garage and Gate Codes:** Provide any gate, garage, or elevator access in your profile."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ACC-001",
    "category": "Property Access & Security",
    "question": "Do I need to be home when the cleaning collective arrives?",
    "answer": "No. Most residential and commercial clients provide a smart lock code or lockbox key. Our technicians arrive within the designated arrival window, complete the service scope, re-secure all windows and doors, and set the security alarm upon departure.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-ACC-002",
    "category": "Property Access & Security",
    "question": "How do you handle my keys and door codes?",
    "answer": "A smart lock with a unique service PIN is preferred. If you use physical keys, they stay in an on-site lockbox, and technicians never carry permanent property keys off-site. Alarm and access codes are stored in encrypted profiles.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "PROPERTY_ACCESS_AND_CANCELLATION_POLICY.md", "quote": "- Report any damage within **24 hours** of service. Photos are required. - Kettle & Loom maintains general liability insurance and bonding. Claims are reviewed within 5 business days. - Fragile and valuable items (jewelry, cash, and collectibles) should be secured. Technicians do not move items worth over $250 unless asked."}]

