}"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MNT-005",
    "category": "Routine Maintenance",
    "question": "How much tread do my tires need in winter?",
    "answer": "At least 4/32 inch for snow traction, even though the legal minimum is 2/32 inch. All-season rubber also hardens below 45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C), so dedicated winter tires are recommended for sub-freezing driving.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WINTERIZATION_AND_PREVENTATIVE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Winter Tread Minimum:** All-season tires require a minimum of **4/32 inch** tread depth for snow traction (replacing normal 2/32 inch legal limit). - **How to Check:** Insert a quarter upside down with Washington's head into the groove. If the top of his head is visible, you are below 4/32 inch. We measure all four tires with a depth gauge during the DVI. - **Compound Glass Transition:** All-season rubber compounds harden below 45°F (7°C), significantly increasing braking distances. Dedicated winter tires recommended for sub-freezing regional travel. - **Winter Tires:** Look for the three-peak mountain snowflake symbol. Install a full set of four, and never just two. - **Tire Pressure:** Pressure drops about **1 psi for every 10°F (5.5°C)** drop in temperature. Check monthly with the tires cold, and set to the pressure on the driver-door placard."}, {"source": "WINTERIZATION_AND_PREVENTATIVE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Blanket, flashlight, jumper cables, ice scraper, small shovel, sand or kitty litter for traction, phone charger, water, and snacks. Keep your fuel tank at least **half full** at all times in winter."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MNT-003",
    "category": "Routine Maintenance",
    "question": "How do you test whether my battery will survive winter?",
    "answer": "We use a conductance tester that compares the battery's output with its rated cold cranking amps (CCA). Batteries that test below 70% of rated CCA are flagged for replacement. Batteries can lose up to 50% of their cranking power below 32\u00b0F (0\u00b0C).",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}]

[{"source": "WARRANTY_AND_REPAIR_POLICY.md", "quote": "- Parts are warranted at the level of the manufacturer or longer. If the manufacturer's warranty is shorter than 24 months, NorthStar covers the remainder of our 24 months or 24,000 miles. - **Consequential Damage:** Towing, rental cars, lost time, and secondary damage are not covered, except where required by law. - Where state law gives you greater rights, the law applies."}, {"source": "WARRANTY_AND_REPAIR_POLICY.md", "quote": "To maintain our strict safety standards and warranty integrity, NorthStar Auto Care *does not install customer-supplied parts*. All parts are sourced through OEM dealership networks or Tier-1 aftermarket suppliers carrying manufacturer warranties. - We can't verify the quality, fitment, or origin of parts we did not source, and we can't warrant labor on a part with no warranty chain. - We will always tell you whether a quoted part is OEM or aftermarket, and show you the price difference. - If you bring a part, we will gladly diagnose the problem, but you will need to use our parts for the repair."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-WAR-001",
    "category": "Warranty & Guarantees",
    "question": "What is covered under the NorthStar Peace of Mind Warranty?",
    "answer": "Our warranty covers parts and labor on qualifying mechanical repairs for 24 months or 24,000 miles, whichever comes first. It is honored at over 35,000 certified facilities nationwide. It excludes customer-supplied parts, routine friction wear (wiper blades, brake pads), commercial severe-duty abuse, racing, and failures from skipped routine fluid maintenance.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-DIAG-006",
    "category": "Diagnostics & Estimates",
    "question": "Can I keep the parts you replace?",
    "answer": "Yes. Ask when you authorize the work and we'll bag and tag them. Exchange items with a core charge, like alternators and starters, can be inspected on-site, but they must be returned to the distributor.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}]

