[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MNT-005",
    "category": "Routine Maintenance",
    "question": "How much tread do my tires need in winter?",
    "answer": "At least 4/32 inch for snow traction, even though the legal minimum is 2/32 inch. All-season rubber also hardens below 45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C), so dedicated winter tires are recommended for sub-freezing driving.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MNT-001",
    "category": "Routine Maintenance",
    "question": "How often should I change my oil?",
    "answer": "Every 5,000 miles or 6 months with a full synthetic oil that meets API SP / ILSAC GF-6 (0W-20 or 5W-30, per your owner's manual). Our Tier 1 service includes the oil and OEM filter, a tire rotation, a brake measurement, and a 40-point inspection for $89.95.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "service_intervals_matrix.json", "quote": "{
    "service": "Tire mount, balance, and install (set of 4)",
    "labor_hours": 1.2
}"}, {"source": "WINTERIZATION_AND_PREVENTATIVE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Winter Tread Minimum:** All-season tires require a minimum of **4/32 inch** tread depth for snow traction (replacing normal 2/32 inch legal limit). - **How to Check:** Insert a quarter upside down with Washington's head into the groove. If the top of his head is visible, you are below 4/32 inch. We measure all four tires with a depth gauge during the DVI. - **Compound Glass Transition:** All-season rubber compounds harden below 45°F (7°C), significantly increasing braking distances. Dedicated winter tires recommended for sub-freezing regional travel. - **Winter Tires:** Look for the three-peak mountain snowflake symbol. Install a full set of four, and never just two. - **Tire Pressure:** Pressure drops about **1 psi for every 10°F (5.5°C)** drop in temperature. Check monthly with the tires cold, and set to the pressure on the driver-door placard."}]

