    "category": "Appointments & Loaners",
    "question": "Can I drop my car off when you are closed?",
    "answer": "Yes. Park in a designated stall, fill out a work order envelope with your name, phone, odometer reading, and a description of the problem, and put the key in the night drop safe at Bay 1. We log your car in the next morning at 6:30 AM, and a service advisor texts you.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "ESTIMATE_AND_AUTHORIZATION_POLICY.md", "quote": "Under state automotive repair regulations, customers retain the right to inspect and keep all replaced mechanical parts (excluding exchange items subject to manufacturer core charges, which may be inspected on-site before return to distributor). - Ask for your old parts when you authorize the work, and we will bag and tag them at the front desk. - Core-charge items (alternators, starters, brake calipers, and batteries) can be viewed on-site, but must go back to the distributor. Core charges are listed on the estimate. - Parts that are hazardous (batteries, fluids) will be handed over in sealed containers or disposed of in compliance with the rules. - Unclaimed parts are kept for **7 days** after pickup of the vehicle."}, {"source": "FLEET_SERVICE_SOP.md", "quote": "1. Secure vehicle in designated Fleet North Lot parking stalls. 2. Complete the Fleet Work Order envelope detailing unit ID, current odometer, and primary driver complaint. 3. Deposit key into the tamper-evident dual-locking night drop safe located at the Bay 1 exterior wall. 4. Early shift dispatch technician logs intake scans into shop management software at **06:30 AM**. **Notes** - Lock the vehicle and remove valuables, fuel cards, and tools. - Any vehicle dropped after-hours is treated as received at 06:30 AM for SLA purposes. It does not qualify for the 08:30 AM same-day PM guarantee unless it was in the lot before 08:30 AM and logged by 06:30 AM, which is the standard case. - NorthStar is not responsible for items left in vehicles. - Our lot is lit and under camera surveillance."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-FLT-005",
    "category": "Fleet Services",
    "question": "Do you service heavy-duty trucks?",
    "answer": "We service commercial light fleets, such as vans and light trucks. Vehicles above 14,000 lb GVWR are outside our standard scope, so please call us to discuss your unit.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MNT-005",
    "category": "Routine Maintenance",
    "question": "How much tread do my tires need in winter?",
    "answer": "At least 4/32 inch for snow traction, even though the legal minimum is 2/32 inch. All-season rubber also hardens below 45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C), so dedicated winter tires are recommended for sub-freezing driving.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "service_intervals_matrix.json", "quote": "{
    "service": "Rear brake pads and rotors, per axle",
    "labor_hours": 1.4
}"}, {"source": "FLEET_SERVICE_SOP.md", "quote": "- **Terms:** Net-30 invoicing upon credit review approval. Credit review takes up to 3 business days. - **Consolidated Monthly Statements:** Itemized billing grouped by vehicle VIN and internal company fleet unit ID. Statements issue on the **1st business day** of each month for the previous month's work, due 30 days from the statement date. - **Late Balances:** Accounts more than 15 days past due may lose priority scheduling until the account is current. A 1.5% monthly finance charge applies after 30 days. - **Disputes:** Raise invoice questions within 14 days of the statement date. - **Warranty:** Fleet repairs receive the same 24-month/24,000-mile warranty as retail repairs, subject to the severe-duty exclusions in `WARRANTY_AND_REPAIR_POLICY.md`."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MNT-005",
    "category": "Routine Maintenance",
    "question": "How much tread do my tires need in winter?",
    "answer": "At least 4/32 inch for snow traction, even though the legal minimum is 2/32 inch. All-season rubber also hardens below 45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C), so dedicated winter tires are recommended for sub-freezing driving.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WINTERIZATION_AND_PREVENTATIVE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Winter Tread Minimum:** All-season tires require a minimum of **4/32 inch** tread depth for snow traction (replacing normal 2/32 inch legal limit). - **How to Check:** Insert a quarter upside down with Washington's head into the groove. If the top of his head is visible, you are below 4/32 inch. We measure all four tires with a depth gauge during the DVI. - **Compound Glass Transition:** All-season rubber compounds harden below 45°F (7°C), significantly increasing braking distances. Dedicated winter tires recommended for sub-freezing regional travel. - **Winter Tires:** Look for the three-peak mountain snowflake symbol. Install a full set of four, and never just two. - **Tire Pressure:** Pressure drops about **1 psi for every 10°F (5.5°C)** drop in temperature. Check monthly with the tires cold, and set to the pressure on the driver-door placard."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MNT-001",
    "category": "Routine Maintenance",
    "question": "How often should I change my oil?",
    "answer": "Every 5,000 miles or 6 months with a full synthetic oil that meets API SP / ILSAC GF-6 (0W-20 or 5W-30, per your owner's manual). Our Tier 1 service includes the oil and OEM filter, a tire rotation, a brake measurement, and a 40-point inspection for $89.95.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WINTERIZATION_AND_PREVENTATIVE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "| Item | What We Do | Why | | :--- | :--- | :--- | | Engine oil | Check viscosity grade (0W-20 or 5W-30 full synthetic as specified) | Thick oil strains the starter | | Wiper blades | Replace if streaking, and use winter-rated washer fluid rated to -20°F (-29°C) | Visibility | | Brake fluid | Test moisture content, replace if above 3% | Moisture freezes and corrodes | | Belts and hoses | Inspect for cracks | Cold makes rubber brittle | | Heater and defroster | Test output | Safety | *Table Summary:* [5. Other Cold-Weather Checks] - Item: Engine oil | What We Do: Check viscosity grade (0W-20 or 5W-30 full synthetic as specified) | Why: Thick oil strains the starter - Item: Wiper blades | What We Do: Replace if streaking, and use winter-rated washer fluid rated to -20°F (-29°C) | Why: Visibility - Item: Brake fluid | What We Do: Test moisture content, replace if above 3% | Why: Moisture freezes and corrodes - Item: Belts and hoses | What We Do: Inspect for cracks | Why: Cold makes rubber brittle - Item: Heater and defroster | What We Do: Test output | Why: Safety"}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-APPT-004",
    "category": "Appointments & Loaners",
    "question": "My car was damaged while at your shop. What do I do?",
    "answer": "Please tell us right away, ideally before leaving the lot, or within 24 hours, and bring photos. A manager reviews every damage report and will contact you.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-WAR-002",
    "category": "Warranty & Guarantees",
    "question": "A part you replaced failed while I'm traveling. What should I do?",
    "answer": "Call us or the nationwide warranty hotline before authorizing any repair, and have your invoice number ready. If you are at a participating facility, they diagnose the failure, and a covered repair costs you $0 for parts and labor.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "WARRANTY_AND_REPAIR_POLICY.md", "quote": "To maintain our strict safety standards and warranty integrity, NorthStar Auto Care *does not install customer-supplied parts*. All parts are sourced through OEM dealership networks or Tier-1 aftermarket suppliers carrying manufacturer warranties. - We can't verify the quality, fitment, or origin of parts we did not source, and we can't warrant labor on a part with no warranty chain. - We will always tell you whether a quoted part is OEM or aftermarket, and show you the price difference. - If you bring a part, we will gladly diagnose the problem, but you will need to use our parts for the repair."}, {"source": "WARRANTY_AND_REPAIR_POLICY.md", "quote": "1. Stop driving the vehicle if the problem is a safety issue (brakes, steering, overheating). 2. Call NorthStar or the nationwide warranty hotline with your original invoice number. 3. Bring the vehicle to NorthStar or a participating facility. A technician will diagnose the failure. If it is covered, you pay $0 for parts and labor. 4. Keep your invoice and the maintenance records for your vehicle, because the warranty requires evidence of routine fluid maintenance. If we need to diagnose a failure that turns out not to be covered, the diagnostic fee applies (see Section 3)."}]

