}"}, {"source": "FLEET_SERVICE_SOP.md", "quote": "1. Secure vehicle in designated Fleet North Lot parking stalls. 2. Complete the Fleet Work Order envelope detailing unit ID, current odometer, and primary driver complaint. 3. Deposit key into the tamper-evident dual-locking night drop safe located at the Bay 1 exterior wall. 4. Early shift dispatch technician logs intake scans into shop management software at **06:30 AM**. **Notes** - Lock the vehicle and remove valuables, fuel cards, and tools. - Any vehicle dropped after-hours is treated as received at 06:30 AM for SLA purposes. It does not qualify for the 08:30 AM same-day PM guarantee unless it was in the lot before 08:30 AM and logged by 06:30 AM, which is the standard case. - NorthStar is not responsible for items left in vehicles. - Our lot is lit and under camera surveillance."}, {"source": "FLEET_SERVICE_SOP.md", "quote": "- **Terms:** Net-30 invoicing upon credit review approval. Credit review takes up to 3 business days. - **Consolidated Monthly Statements:** Itemized billing grouped by vehicle VIN and internal company fleet unit ID. Statements issue on the **1st business day** of each month for the previous month's work, due 30 days from the statement date. - **Late Balances:** Accounts more than 15 days past due may lose priority scheduling until the account is current. A 1.5% monthly finance charge applies after 30 days. - **Disputes:** Raise invoice questions within 14 days of the statement date. - **Warranty:** Fleet repairs receive the same 24-month/24,000-mile warranty as retail repairs, subject to the severe-duty exclusions in `WARRANTY_AND_REPAIR_POLICY.md`."}]

[{"source": "WINTERIZATION_AND_PREVENTATIVE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "Blanket, flashlight, jumper cables, ice scraper, small shovel, sand or kitty litter for traction, phone charger, water, and snacks. Keep your fuel tank at least **half full** at all times in winter."}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MNT-004",
    "category": "Routine Maintenance",
    "question": "What coolant mix protects against freezing?",
    "answer": "A 50/50 mix of ethylene glycol and deionized water protects to -34\u00b0F (-37\u00b0C). We check it with a hydrometer. Richer than about 70% glycol actually freezes at a higher temperature, so more is not better. Don't mix different coolant types.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-MNT-003",
    "category": "Routine Maintenance",
    "question": "How do you test whether my battery will survive winter?",
    "answer": "We use a conductance tester that compares the battery's output with its rated cold cranking amps (CCA). Batteries that test below 70% of rated CCA are flagged for replacement. Batteries can lose up to 50% of their cranking power below 32\u00b0F (0\u00b0C).",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "WINTERIZATION_AND_PREVENTATIVE_GUIDE.md", "quote": "- **Concentration Target:** 50/50 ethylene glycol and deionized water mixture providing freeze protection down to **-34°F (-37°C)**. - **Testing:** A hydrometer or refractometer measures the density of the coolant, and the reading is converted to a freeze point. We test it when the engine is cool. - **Pass:** -34°F (-37°C) or lower. - **Marginal:** -20°F to -33°F (-29°C to -36°C), so we top off with proper mix or flush the system. - **Fail:** Warmer than -20°F (-29°C). Flush and refill. - **Don't Overconcentrate:** A mix richer than 70% glycol freezes at a *higher* temperature and transfers heat less effectively. Use pre-mixed or deionized water, and never tap water. - **Boil-over & Corrosion Protection:** Fresh inhibitor additives prevent internal engine block scale and heater core pinhole corrosion. A 50/50 mix also raises the boiling point to about 265°F (129°C) with a 15 psi cap. - **Coolant Types:** Use the type your manufacturer requires (OAT, HOAT, or IAT) and don't mix types, because they can gel. Typical service interval is 30,000 to 60,000 miles for extended-life coolants (see `service_intervals_matrix.json`)."}]

[{"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-FLT-005",
    "category": "Fleet Services",
    "question": "Do you service heavy-duty trucks?",
    "answer": "We service commercial light fleets, such as vans and light trucks. Vehicles above 14,000 lb GVWR are outside our standard scope, so please call us to discuss your unit.",
    "escalate_to_human": true
}"}, {"source": "faq_support_knowledgebase.json", "quote": "{
    "id": "FAQ-FLT-001",
    "category": "Fleet Services",
    "question": "How do after-hours drop-offs work for commercial fleet vehicles?",
    "answer": "Park your vehicle in our secure Fleet North Lot stalls, complete the Fleet Work Order envelope with your fleet unit number, odometer, and driver notes, and place the key in the tamper-evident dual-locking drop safe at the Bay 1 exterior wall. Our early-shift dispatch team logs all vehicles at 6:30 AM for priority triage.",
    "escalate_to_human": false
}"}, {"source": "FLEET_SERVICE_SOP.md", "quote": "Fleet vehicles are placed ahead of retail customers in the bay queue, with safety-critical repairs and customer emergencies taking precedence over both. | Service Class | Examples | Guaranteed Turnaround | | :--- | :--- | :--- | | **Preventative Maintenance (PM Tier 1)** | Oil and filter, tire rotation, DVI | **Same-day (under 4 hours)** for drop-offs before **08:30 AM** | | **Minor Mechanical Repairs** | Brakes, alternators, starters | **24-hour** turnaround window | | **Major Drivetrain Overhauls** | Transmission, engine, differential | Dedicated bay allocation with **daily status progress dispatch** | *Table Summary:* [1. Priority Triage & Guaranteed Turnaround (SLA)] - Service Class: Preventative Maintenance (PM Tier 1) | Examples: Oil and filter, tire rotation, DVI | Guaranteed Turnaround: Same-day (under 4 hours) for drop-offs before 08:30 AM - Service Class: Minor Mechanical Repairs | Examples: Brakes, alternators, starters | Guaranteed Turnaround: 24-hour turnaround window - Service Class: Major Drivetrain Overhauls | Examples: Transmission, engine, differential | Guaranteed Turnaround: Dedicated bay allocation with daily status progress dispatch - **Clock Start:** The SLA clock starts when the vehicle is logged into our shop management software, and when diagnostic approval is received if the repair requires authorization above the pre-authorized limit. - **Parts Delays:** If a part is not available locally, we source it from a second supplier. We notify the fleet manager within **2 hours** of discovering a delay, with the new estimated completion time. - **SLA Exceptions:** Delays caused by awaiting approval, hidden damage found during teardown (see `ESTIMATE_AND_AUTHORIZATION_POLICY.md`), or manufacturer back-orders pause the SLA clock. - **Severe-Duty Notice:** Vehicles with GVWR above 14,000 lb are outside the light-fleet scope. Call us to discuss them."}]

